window.SAND_QUIZ = {"0":{"file":"00-overview","questions":[{"q":"A polished 300 mm prime wafer costs ~$100-150 when it enters a leading-edge fab and is worth ~$17,000-20,000 when it leaves ~3 months later. Which single factor best explains where that value was added?","options":["The silicon was purified further inside the fab, from 9N to 11N","Roughly 1,000-1,500 process steps across ~70-100+ mask layers were performed on tools from a fab costing ~$20-30B, with most capex in equipment","The wafer was thinned from 775 um to ~30 um, which is the most expensive step","The fab embedded HBM memory into the wafer during the BEOL"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The fab adds value through the ~1,000+ patterning, deposition, etch, implant, and CMP steps that build transistors and ~15-18 wiring levels, amortizing a $20-30B facility whose cost is ~70-80% equipment. Purity is fixed before the fab; thinning and HBM integration happen afterward, in packaging."},{"q":"In the module's illustrative H100 SXM bill-of-materials model, which listed component contributes the largest estimated hardware cost?","options":["The 814 mm2 GPU die (~$350 at ~45-50 sellable die per ~$16.5k wafer)","The five HBM3 stacks (80 GB at ~$15-20/GB, roughly $1,200-1,600)","Wafer sort and final test","The SXM printed circuit board and voltage regulators"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In this illustrative model, HBM has the largest listed component cost. These are estimates for a historical configuration, not disclosed purchasing prices or a measured NVIDIA product gross margin."},{"q":"TSMC reports total wafer capacity across many nodes. Why does that number alone fail to identify the bottleneck for a particular AI accelerator?","options":["All process nodes and package types are interchangeable","A package needs appropriately allocated logic wafers, memory and packaging capacity; the smallest compatible output can constrain shipments","Only the raw silicon mass matters","Foundry capacity measures finished GPU racks"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Total 12-inch-equivalent capacity does not disclose the relevant node, allocation, yield or package and memory availability. Bottlenecks must be assessed for the actual product and period."},{"q":"A planning example assumes 100,000 wafer starts per month, 24 EUV passes per wafer, and 100,000 productive passes per scanner per month. How many scanners cover that nominal workload?","options":["4","12","24","240"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The demand is 2,400,000 passes per month. Divide by 100,000 productive passes per scanner to obtain 24 scanners. Real plans additionally account for the assumptions behind availability, dose, maintenance and reserve capacity."},{"q":"Which set correctly names the three chokepoints of the leading-edge supply chain as described in this module?","options":["Quartz mining, metallurgical-grade silicon smelting, and polysilicon purification","EUV lithography (ASML), leading-edge foundry (TSMC in Taiwan), and advanced packaging plus HBM (CoWoS; SK hynix/Samsung/Micron)","EDA software, CPU IP licensing, and server ODM assembly","Copper supply, ultrapure water, and data-center electricity"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each of these three is a step where one or a handful of firms hold nearly all capability: ASML is the sole EUV supplier, TSMC makes >90% of the most advanced logic wafers, and CoWoS capacity and HBM output gated AI accelerator supply. Raw silicon is cheap and abundant."},{"q":"In the master process flow, 'replacement metal gate' (gate-last) comes AFTER which of these steps, and why?","options":["After M1 dual damascene, because the gate is formed in the BEOL","After source/drain epitaxy and ILD0 CMP, because the dummy polysilicon gate must survive the high-temperature S/D epi and anneal steps before being swapped for high-k and work-function metals","Before STI formation, because the gate defines the active area","After wafer sort, because the gate metal is deposited during bumping"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a gate-last flow, a sacrificial amorphous-Si dummy gate is patterned first, spacers and embedded S/D epi are formed around it, ILD0 is deposited and polished to expose it, and only then is it removed and replaced with the HfO2 high-k stack, work-function metals, and W/Co fill, so the metals never see the S/D thermal budget."},{"q":"A component BOM is estimated at $3,000 and a reseller quotes $30,000 for a GPU module. Which conclusion is justified?","options":["NVIDIA has a verified 90% product gross margin","NVIDIA spends $27,000 on R&D per unit","The component estimate is 10% of the reseller quote, but realized revenue and full cost of revenue are needed to calculate gross margin","The silicon wafer costs $30,000 per die"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A BOM can omit manufacturing overhead, testing, warranty and other costs; the reseller quote need not equal manufacturer revenue. The arithmetic gap is not a disclosed margin."},{"q":"A GPU and its HBM stacks need thousands of wires between them in a strip a few millimetres wide. Which carrier can route wiring at the ~1 um pitch this requires, and what is it called in a CoWoS package?","options":["The printed circuit board (~50-100 um line pitch), called the baseboard","The ABF build-up substrate (~10 um pitch), called the core","A slab of silicon wiring with no transistors (~1 um pitch), called the interposer","The copper NVLink spine (~1 mm pitch), called the bridge"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Every carrier has a minimum wire spacing: a PCB ~50-100 um, an ABF substrate ~10 um, a silicon interposer made with fab lithography ~1 um, the chip itself <0.1 um. Only silicon-class patterning can route thousands of GPU-to-HBM wires in a few millimetres, so a silicon (or fine-RDL) interposer sits between the dies and the organic substrate; that is the \"wafer\" in Chip-on-Wafer-on-Substrate."}]},"1":{"file":"01-sand-to-polysilicon","questions":[{"q":"Silicon contains about 5 x 10^22 atoms per cm3. A polysilicon lot is certified at 9N (99.9999999%) total purity. Roughly how many impurity atoms per cm3 does that correspond to, and how does it compare with the ~1.3 x 10^15 cm-3 of boron a wafer maker adds on purpose for a 10 ohm-cm p-type substrate?","options":["5 x 10^16 cm-3, about 40 times more than the intentional doping","5 x 10^13 cm-3, about 25 times fewer than the intentional doping","5 x 10^10 cm-3, about 25,000 times fewer than the intentional doping","5 x 10^19 cm-3, roughly equal to the intentional doping"],"answer":1,"explanation":"9N means 1 part per billion, so 5 x 10^22 x 10^-9 = 5 x 10^13 impurity atoms per cm3, about 25 times below the deliberate boron level of 1.3 x 10^15 cm-3; only a small fraction of those impurities are electrically active dopants."},{"q":"Why does the Siemens process go to such lengths (many-stage distillation, resin guard beds, complexing agents) to remove boron from trichlorosilane, when metals like iron receive comparatively little attention at that stage?","options":["Boron trichloride is a solid at column temperatures and clogs the packing, while metal chlorides are volatile","Boron has a segregation coefficient of ~0.8 in silicon so crystal growth cannot reject it, and BCl3 boils only ~19 C below SiHCl3, making it hard to separate; iron (k ~ 10^-5) is rejected into the melt during crystal growth and FeCl3 is non-volatile anyway","Boron is the only impurity that survives the 1,100 C deposition step; all other impurities burn off as gases","Iron is removed earlier in the arc furnace by oxidative ladle refining, so none reaches the chlorosilane plant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Boron goes almost entirely into the crystal during Czochralski or float-zone growth (k ~ 0.8), so it must be removed chemically, yet BCl3 (12.5 C) and SiHCl3 (31.8 C) have similar volatility. Metals segregate strongly to the melt and their chlorides stay in the column bottoms."},{"q":"A modern Siemens deposition reactor consumes 40-60 kWh per kg of silicon, and older ones 100-150 kWh/kg, even though the reaction SiHCl3 + H2 -> Si + 3HCl is only mildly endothermic. Where does most of the energy go?","options":["Compressing hydrogen to the 5-6 bar operating pressure","Breaking the Si-Cl bonds, which are stronger than Si-O bonds","Thermal radiation from the ~1,100 C rods to the water-cooled bell-jar wall, which must stay cold so silicon does not deposit on it","Heating the slim rods from room temperature to 400 C at start-up, since cold silicon is nearly an insulator"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A grey body at 1,100 C radiates on the order of 140 kW per square meter, and the reactor wall is deliberately kept cold; rod count, pressure, reflective (silver-plated) walls and the FBR alternative all exist to reduce that radiative loss."},{"q":"The carbothermic reduction SiO2 + 2C -> Si + 2CO has an enthalpy of about +690 kJ per mole of silicon. Which statement about the submerged-arc furnace is correct?","options":["The theoretical minimum is ~6.8 MWh per tonne of silicon and real furnaces use 11-13 MWh/t, so about 55-60% of the electricity goes into the reaction","Real furnaces use about 2 MWh/t because the reaction becomes exothermic above 1,800 C","The furnace is fuel-fired with natural gas because arcs cannot reach 2,000 C","Fine beach sand is preferred as feed because its high surface area speeds the reaction"],"answer":0,"explanation":"690 kJ/mol divided by 28.09 g/mol gives 24.6 MJ/kg = 6.8 kWh/kg; the remainder of the 11-13 MWh/t covers heating the charge to ~2,000 C, melting, and heat carried out by CO gas. Lumpy quartz is required so gases can escape the packed bed."},{"q":"Why does a large solar-polysilicon producer not automatically become a qualified electronic-grade supplier for every 300 mm wafer process?","options":["FBR chemistry cannot produce semiconductor-purity silicon, regardless of process control","Electronic-grade supply needs stringent impurity and surface-cleanliness control, verification and customer qualification; solar tonnage alone does not demonstrate those requirements","Every semiconductor customer requires its polysilicon to be manufactured in the same country as its wafer factory","Granular silicon cannot be loaded into a Czochralski crucible"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The relevant test is the material and its qualification for a particular process. Xinhua Semiconductor is one example of an electronic-grade supplier. Solar output volume does not establish electronic-grade purity, consistency or customer acceptance."},{"q":"A 300 mm Czochralski ingot has a 2 m cylindrical body grown at ~306 mm diameter. Using a silicon density of 2.33 g/cm3 and a per-wafer pitch of ~1.0-1.1 mm (775 um wafer plus kerf and polishing losses), which estimate is correct?","options":["About 34 kg of body silicon and ~180 wafers","About 343 kg of body silicon and ~1,800-2,000 wafers, so roughly 0.2-0.25 kg of polysilicon per shipped wafer once the charge, crown, tail and pot scrap are counted","About 3,400 kg of body silicon and ~20,000 wafers","About 343 kg of body silicon and ~2,600 wafers, since kerf is negligible with diamond wire"],"answer":1,"explanation":"pi x (15.3 cm)^2 x 200 cm = ~147,000 cm3, times 2.33 g/cm3 = ~343 kg; a 2,000 mm body at ~1.05 mm pitch gives ~1,900 slices. With a ~400 kg charge and ~1,700 shipped wafers, that is ~0.23 kg polysilicon per wafer, of which only ~127 g is in the finished wafer."},{"q":"Boron trichloride boils at 12.5 C and trichlorosilane at 31.8 C, a relative volatility of roughly 2. Why do the TCS distillation columns need 60-100 trays to bring boron from ~10 ppm in crude TCS down to ~10 ppt?","options":["Because BCl3 is a solid at column temperature and must be filtered off on every tray","Because cutting boron by six orders of magnitude at alpha ~ 2 needs ln(10^6)/ln 2 ~ 20 theoretical stages even at total reflux, roughly double that at a practical reflux ratio, and a real tray is only ~0.5-0.7 of a theoretical stage","Because each tray removes exactly 1% of the boron, so 100 trays remove all of it","Because the trays are needed to strip iron and aluminum chlorides, which are more volatile than TCS"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The Fenske equation gives N_min = ln F / ln alpha = ln(10^6)/ln 2 ~ 20 stages at total reflux; a practical reflux ratio roughly doubles that, and at a tray efficiency of ~0.5-0.7 that is ~60-80 real trays. Boron side-species that do not obey ideal volatility are why chemical complexing, guard beds and a generous lights cut are layered on top of the columns."},{"q":"Fluidized-bed (FBR) granular polysilicon made from silane uses only 5-15 kWh/kg against 40-60 kWh/kg for a modern Siemens reactor and runs continuously. Why has it not displaced Siemens chunk as feedstock for 300 mm electronic-grade crystals?","options":["Silane cannot be purified below 6N because boron and phosphorus hydrides boil at the same temperature as silane","Granules collide constantly with the reactor wall and pick up iron, chromium and nickel on their surfaces, and they trap tens of ppm of hydrogen that bubbles out and splashes silicon when they melt in a CZ crucible; the material is therefore used as a 20-50% blend in solar crucibles, not as electronic-grade feed","FBR granules are too large to fit through the feed tube of a Czochralski puller","The FBR route uses TCS, whose 31.8 C boiling point makes it unsuitable for a continuous reactor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Wall metals and trapped hydrogen are the two unsolved contamination problems, which is why granular silicon is a solar blend material; REC Silicon's Moses Lake granular product failed Hanwha's qualification on impurity levels and ingot yield in 2024-25. Silane itself is easier to purify than TCS, since B2H6 (-93 C) and PH3 (-88 C) boil far from silane's -112 C."}]},"2":{"file":"02-crystal-growth","questions":[{"q":"Why does the Dash necking process eliminate dislocations from a Czochralski seed crystal?","options":["The high temperature of the melt anneals the dislocations out of the seed before growth begins","Growing a thin (~3 mm) neck at several mm/min lets dislocations, which glide on {111} planes inclined to the <100> axis, reach the free surface and exit before the crystal carries them along","The neck is grown under a strong magnetic field that pins dislocations in the seed holder","Dislocations cannot exist in silicon below a critical diameter of about 5 mm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Dash's method is geometric: in a thin, fast-grown neck every dislocation inclined to the growth axis glides out to the surface within a few tens of millimetres, and the vacancy supersaturation from fast growth helps them climb out. After that the crystal stays dislocation-free unless a new one is nucleated."},{"q":"A 300 mm CZ ingot is grown from a 400 kg boron-doped melt with k = 0.8, targeted at 10 ohm-cm at the seed end. According to the Scheil equation, what happens to the resistivity toward the tail, and why?","options":["It rises, because boron evaporates from the melt during the run","It stays exactly constant, because the melt is well mixed","It falls to roughly 6 to 7 ohm-cm at 90% solidified, because k < 1 leaves the melt progressively enriched in boron and C_s = k C0 (1-g)^(k-1)","It falls by a factor of about 4.5, the same as phosphorus"],"answer":2,"explanation":"With k = 0.8 the exponent (k-1) is -0.2, so the solid concentration at g = 0.9 is 0.1^(-0.2) = 1.58 times the seed-end value, taking ~10 ohm-cm down to ~6.4 ohm-cm. Phosphorus (k = 0.35) would give a factor of ~4.5, which is why n-type CZ ingots have a much larger axial spread."},{"q":"In Voronkov's v/G theory, what determines whether a CZ crystal becomes vacancy-rich (forming COP voids) or interstitial-rich (forming dislocation loops)?","options":["The oxygen concentration: above 12 ppma the crystal is vacancy-rich","The ratio of pull rate v to the axial temperature gradient G at the interface; above a critical value of about 1.3 to 2 x 10^-3 cm^2/(min K) advection dominates and the more numerous vacancies arrive in excess and annihilate the interstitials, while below it diffusion dominates and the faster-diffusing interstitials win","The crucible rotation rate, which sets the convective flux of interstitials to the interface","The dopant type: boron-doped crystals are always vacancy-rich"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Both point defects are incorporated at equilibrium and then annihilate, diffuse, and are advected at speed v. High v/G favours advection and leaves excess vacancies (COPs); low v/G gives diffusion time and the faster interstitials win. Because G is higher at the crystal edge, one pull rate can give both regimes across the radius, separated by the OSF ring."},{"q":"Why are the interstitial oxygen levels of 10 to 14 ppma found in CZ silicon deliberately retained in most logic and memory substrates rather than minimised?","options":["Oxygen is an n-type dopant that sets the substrate resistivity","Oxygen cannot be removed because it comes from the argon atmosphere","Dissolved oxygen strengthens the wafer against slip during thermal processing, and controlled oxygen precipitation in the bulk provides intrinsic gettering of metals beneath a denuded zone","Oxygen lowers the melting point of the melt so pulling can proceed at a lower heater power"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Oxygen from crucible dissolution pins dislocations (raising yield stress) and, via a denuded-zone / precipitation anneal sequence, forms SiO2 precipitates and punched-out loops in the bulk that trap Fe, Cu and Ni. Only image sensors, power and RF products specify low oxygen, using MCZ or FZ."},{"q":"A 300 kg ingot hangs from a 3 mm Dash neck. Roughly what tensile stress does the neck carry, and what does the industry do about it at 300 mm?","options":["About 4 MPa, which is negligible, so no special measures are needed","About 40 MPa, handled by using a steel seed holder","About 400 MPa, so pullers use thicker fast-grown necks, heavily boron-doped neck-less seeds, or mechanical crystal-support jaws that grip the crown after it is grown","About 4 GPa, which is why 300 mm ingots are limited to 100 kg"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Stress = m g / (pi r^2) = 2,943 N / 7.07 x 10^-6 m^2, about 416 MPa, uncomfortably close to practical limits for a real neck with surface flaws. Crystal support mechanisms, thicker necks and hardened (B > 10^19) seeds are the production solutions."},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes Float Zone silicon relative to Czochralski silicon?","options":["FZ uses a synthetic quartz crucible with a stronger magnetic field, giving lower oxygen and 300 mm capability","FZ has no crucible, so oxygen is below ~10^16 cm^-3 and resistivity can exceed 10^4 ohm-cm, but surface-tension support of the molten zone limits production diameter to 200 mm and the oxygen-free wafers are mechanically weaker in the fab","FZ is cheaper than CZ because it needs no crucible, and is used for all DRAM substrates","FZ crystals are polycrystalline and used only for solar cells"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In FZ an RF coil melts a short zone in a vertical poly rod, held by surface tension and electromagnetic pressure, with no container to leach oxygen. It serves power devices, detectors and high-resistivity RF substrates, at a price multiple of CZ and with a 200 mm production ceiling."},{"q":"A crystal grower increases pull speed while the axial temperature gradient stays fixed. Which control ratio changes directly?","options":["The v/G ratio increases","The silicon melting point rises","The wafer diameter must double","The segregation coefficient becomes exactly one"],"answer":0,"explanation":"v is pull speed and G is temperature gradient. Their ratio affects the balance of intrinsic point defects; faster output must be checked against the defect-control window."},{"q":"Why does float-zone growth usually introduce less oxygen than conventional CZ growth?","options":["It uses a colder solid throughout","Its molten zone does not contact a quartz crucible","The silicon contains no oxygen atoms before growth under any circumstances","It eliminates the need for a seed crystal"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A quartz crucible supplies oxygen to a CZ melt. Avoiding that contact removes an important oxygen source; it does not imply absolute zero contamination."}]},"3":{"file":"03-ingot-to-wafer","questions":[{"q":"A 300 mm block is sliced on a diamond-wire saw with a 120 µm core wire carrying ~15 µm grit, producing as-cut slices of ~900 µm. Roughly how much ingot length is consumed per wafer, and what fraction of that ends up as kerf sludge?","options":["About 0.90 mm per wafer, with essentially no kerf because diamond wire cuts without loss","About 1.06 mm per wafer, of which ~160 µm (~15%) is kerf","About 1.5 mm per wafer, of which ~600 µm (40%) is kerf","About 0.78 mm per wafer, since the saw cuts directly to the final 775 µm thickness"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Pitch equals as-cut thickness plus kerf; the kerf is roughly the wire diameter plus twice the grit height, ~160 µm, so each wafer consumes ~1.06 mm of ingot and ~15% of that is lost as sludge. The slice is later ground, etched and polished from ~900 µm down to 775 µm."},{"q":"Why does chemical-mechanical polishing with colloidal silica leave no subsurface damage layer, when even the finest diamond grinding does?","options":["The silica particles are so small that their cracks are below the resolution of any metrology","The polishing pressure is too low for any fracture to occur, so removal is purely mechanical but gentle","The alkaline slurry chemically hydrates the top silicon monolayers, and the silica abrasive (softer than silicon) only shears away that weakened film, so the crystal is never fractured","The polyurethane pad absorbs all the mechanical energy, leaving the slurry to dissolve silicon uniformly"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CMP removal is chemical-mechanical: pH 10-11 slurry softens the surface into a hydrated silica-like film and the ~50 nm amorphous silica particles (Mohs ~6 vs silicon ~7) remove only that film. Since nothing harder than silicon touches the crystal, no cracks or dislocations are created."},{"q":"Which statement correctly describes the roles of SC-1 and SC-2 in the RCA clean?","options":["SC-1 (HCl/H2O2) removes metals; SC-2 (NH4OH/H2O2) removes particles","SC-1 (NH4OH/H2O2) undercuts and lifts particles by repeatedly growing and etching a chemical oxide, but can deposit metal hydroxides at high pH, which SC-2 (HCl/H2O2) then dissolves as chlorides","Both are HF-based; SC-1 is dilute and SC-2 is concentrated, removing successively thicker oxides","SC-1 removes organics only; SC-2 removes both particles and metals, so SC-1 is optional"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SC-1's particle removal works by oxide regrowth/etch undercut plus electrostatic repulsion at pH ~10, but Fe, Al, Zn and Ca precipitate as hydroxides in that bath. The acidic SC-2 keeps those metals in solution as soluble chlorides while peroxide holds them oxidized so they do not plate onto the silicon."},{"q":"A polished 300 mm wafer is specified at SFQR of 20 nm, yet the same wafer sags roughly 90 µm at its center when supported only at the rim. How are these compatible?","options":["The 90 µm figure applies only to 200 mm wafers; 300 mm wafers are thick enough not to sag","SFQR is measured with the back surface chucked flat and is the residual range within each 26 x 8 mm site after a best-fit plane, so gravity sag and global shape are removed just as a scanner's per-site leveling removes them","The wafer maker pre-warps the wafer so that gravity flattens it during exposure","SFQR is only specified in the central 100 mm of the wafer where sag is negligible"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Flatness metrics such as SFQR are defined for a back-chucked wafer and per lithography site, so long-wavelength shape (sag, bow, warp) is filtered out. The scanner tilts and focuses on each site, and only the residual within the site limits its focus budget."},{"q":"Why does an epitaxial layer grown by CVD from trichlorosilane at ~1,100 °C eliminate crystal-originated particles (COPs) from the wafer surface?","options":["The high temperature melts the top few micrometers of the substrate and recrystallizes it without voids","HCl produced by the TCS reaction etches away all the voids before growth starts","The epi layer is grown atom by atom from the gas phase with no vacancy supersaturation, so it contains no voids, and lateral step-flow growth overgrows the ~100 nm substrate pits within the first few hundred nanometers","COPs migrate toward the heavily doped p+ substrate during the hydrogen bake"],"answer":2,"explanation":"COPs are ~100 nm octahedral voids from vacancy agglomeration during crystal growth. A CVD layer is deposited under conditions that never create such vacancy clusters, and the growing steps fill in the pits where substrate voids intersect the surface, giving a defect-free device layer."},{"q":"In the Smart Cut SOI process, what actually causes the donor wafer to split, transferring a thin silicon film onto the handle?","options":["The buried oxide is selectively etched with HF, releasing the top layer","A high-dose hydrogen implant (~5 x 10^16 cm^-2) creates a buried layer of platelets that grow, pressurize and coalesce during a 400-600 °C anneal, fracturing the donor along the implant plane after it has been bonded to the handle","The bonded pair is cooled rapidly so thermal-expansion mismatch cleaves the donor","A wire saw slices the bonded pair at the depth of the implant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Hydrogen implanted at a chosen energy defines the split depth with implant-level uniformity; the anneal after room-temperature hydrophilic bonding drives platelet growth until the donor cleaves. The transferred film is then annealed at ~1,100 °C to form covalent bonds and thinned/smoothed to its final thickness, and the donor is reused ~10 times."},{"q":"A 300 mm wafer leaves double-disk grinding with TTV below 1 µm. Why do wafer makers remove the grinding damage with a hot KOH (alkaline) etch rather than a faster HF/HNO3 acid etch, even though the acid leaves a smoother, glossier surface?","options":["KOH is cheaper and produces no waste, so the flatness difference is incidental","The KOH reaction is reaction-rate-limited, so every point on the wafer etches at the rate the surface chemistry allows regardless of liquid flow and the grinder's flatness is preserved; the acid etch is diffusion-limited, so edges and corners that stick out into fresh acid etch faster and TTV grows by several micrometres","The acid etch cannot dissolve the subsurface cracks, only the KOH can reach them","KOH etches isotropically, which keeps the surface flat, while the acid etch is anisotropic and forms facets"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a diffusion-limited etch the chemistry outruns the supply of fresh reagent, so the rate is set by how the liquid moves and protruding regions thin faster; in a reaction-limited etch reagent is always in excess and the rate is set by the surface chemistry alone. That single distinction is why alkaline etching preserves the flatness made in grinding (at the cost of a matte, (111)-faceted surface that polishing later removes) while acid etching, though smoother, degrades TTV and thins the edge."},{"q":"In a multi-wire saw the wire tension is held to within a fraction of a newton over a cut lasting many hours. Why does tension control determine the thickness uniformity of the slices?","options":["Higher tension makes the wire thinner, so the kerf and therefore the slice thickness change directly with tension","The block pushes each strand sideways so it bows by a few millimetres, and the tension acting through that bow is the cutting force; if the tension, feed or block temperature changes, the bow changes, the plane in which the wire cuts moves, and the slice thickness varies along the cut","Tension sets the wire speed, and faster wire cuts thicker slices","Tension has no effect on thickness; it only prevents the wire from breaking"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The wire acts like a bowstring: the sideways force on the block is roughly 2T sin α, where T is the tension and α the bow angle. Any drift in tension or feed, or thermal expansion of the block during a multi-hour cut, changes the bow and shifts the cutting plane, producing as-cut TTV of 10–20 µm and warp of 20–40 µm that the grinder must then correct."}]},"4":{"file":"04-materials-and-consumables","questions":[{"q":"Silicon carbide boules are grown by physical vapor transport (seeded sublimation) rather than by Czochralski pulling. What is the fundamental reason?","options":["SiC is too hard to be pulled through a seed neck without cracking","Quartz crucibles dissolve carbon, contaminating the melt","SiC never melts into a liquid of its own composition at practical pressures (it has no congruent melting point); it decomposes at ~2,830 °C and 35 atm into liquid silicon and solid carbon, so there is no stable melt to pull from","The 4H polytype can only form from a vapor phase below 1,000 °C"],"answer":2,"explanation":"SiC sublimes and decomposes rather than melting under accessible conditions, so it must be transported as vapor from a ~2,400 °C source onto a slightly cooler seed at ~100–500 µm/h, which is why boules take about a week and substrates cost tens of times more than a silicon wafer of the same diameter."},{"q":"A Si0.75Ge0.25 layer is grown epitaxially on a silicon wafer. Which statement about its strain and critical thickness is correct?","options":["The film is under ~1% compressive strain (mismatch ≈ 0.042 x 0.25) and relaxes by misfit dislocations above an equilibrium critical thickness of roughly 35–40 nm","The film is under tensile strain because Ge atoms are smaller than Si, and it can be grown arbitrarily thick","The film is unstrained because SiGe is fully miscible with Si","The film relaxes only if its Ge content exceeds 50%, regardless of thickness"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Ge's lattice constant is 4.2% larger than Si's, so the alloy is compressed in-plane to match the substrate; Matthews–Blakeslee theory gives a critical thickness of a few tens of nm at x = 0.25, which is why nanosheet SiGe sacrificial layers are kept to ~10 nm."},{"q":"Why does an AlGaN/GaN HEMT conduct without any intentional doping of the channel?","options":["GaN is naturally n-type due to nitrogen vacancies at ~10^19 cm^-3","Aluminum diffuses into the GaN and acts as a shallow donor","The silicon substrate injects electrons through the buffer layer","Spontaneous and piezoelectric polarization at the AlGaN/GaN interface create a fixed sheet charge that is neutralized by a ~10^13 cm^-2 two-dimensional electron gas with mobility of ~1,500–2,000 cm^2/V·s"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The wurtzite polarization discontinuity forms a 2DEG confined in a few-nm quantum well, spatially separated from any ionized donors, which is what gives the HEMT its low sheet resistance; a p-GaN gate or cascode is then needed to make the device normally off."},{"q":"A fab producing 100,000 300 mm wafers per month uses about 1,800 gallons of ultrapure water per wafer. Roughly how much UPW must its plant produce per day, and what is the theoretical resistivity the water must reach? Assume a 30-day month and water at 25 °C.","options":["About 600,000 gallons/day at 1.8 MΩ·cm","About 6 million gallons/day at 18.2 MΩ·cm","About 60 million gallons/day at 18.2 MΩ·cm","About 6 million gallons/day at 182 MΩ·cm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"100,000 x 1,800 / 30 ≈ 6.0 million gallons/day (~22,700 m^3/day). 18.2 MΩ·cm at 25 °C is the resistivity of water with essentially zero ions; with TOC below 1 ppb and metals below 1 ppt it is the purest liquid produced industrially, and ~85–90% of it is reclaimed at leading fabs."},{"q":"Which set correctly describes an EUV photomask blank and why only two companies supply it?","options":["A 6025 fused-silica plate with a 70 nm chromium absorber; Hoya and AGC hold patents on chrome sputtering","A quartz plate with a MoSi 6% attenuated phase shifter; Hoya and AGC are the only makers of molybdenum silicide","A low-thermal-expansion (ULE-type) substrate with 40 Mo/Si bilayers (~7 nm period, ~65% reflectivity), a Ru cap and a TaBN absorber; Hoya and AGC are the only makers who can deposit the multilayer with essentially zero printable phase defects","A silicon wafer coated with a thin tin-oxide absorber; only Hoya and AGC have licensed the Inpria chemistry"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Because everything absorbs 13.5 nm light, the EUV mask is a Bragg mirror; a particle buried in any of the 40 layers becomes an unrepairable phase defect, and the ion-beam deposition yield needed to avoid that is what has kept Hoya (~2/3) and AGC (~1/3) as an effective duopoly."},{"q":"A pellicle transmits 90% of EUV light per pass. What is its double-pass optical transmission?","options":["90%","81%","19%","180%"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Light crosses it toward the reflective mask and again on the return path: 0.9 × 0.9 = 0.81. The resulting 19% optical loss is not automatically a 19% full-cycle throughput loss, because handling and stage overhead also consume time."},{"q":"A water monitor reads 18.2 MΩ·cm at 25 °C. What additional checks are still necessary for fab use?","options":["None; this certifies every contaminant is absent","Only water color","Independent checks of particles, organic carbon and relevant trace contaminants","Only reservoir size"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Resistivity is primarily sensitive to ions. An excellent reading does not measure every form of contamination."},{"q":"Why must a nominally equivalent replacement slurry undergo process qualification?","options":["Its label determines the final transistor design","Particle distribution, chemistry and selectivity can change removal rates and defectivity","Only the supplier's nationality affects polishing","Slurries never interact with existing films"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Chemical-mechanical polishing combines chemical reactions and abrasion. A replacement must preserve removal, uniformity and defect performance on the actual film stack."}]},"5":{"file":"05-inside-a-fab","questions":[{"q":"A fab starts 100,000 wafers per month and has a 90-day cycle time. Using Little's law, roughly how many wafers are in the fab (WIP) at any instant?","options":["About 30,000 wafers","About 100,000 wafers","About 300,000 wafers","About 3,000,000 wafers"],"answer":2,"explanation":"WIP = throughput x cycle time. 100,000 wafers/month is ~3,300 wafers/day; times 90 days gives ~296,000 wafers, i.e. roughly 12,000 lots of 25 sitting in tools, buffers, and stockers."},{"q":"Why can the ballroom of a 300 mm leading-edge fab be run at only ISO 5-6 rather than ISO 1-3?","options":["Modern ULPA filters make the whole room ISO 1 anyway, so the ballroom class is only a nominal rating","Wafers are sealed in FOUPs at all times and are only exposed inside tool mini-environments (EFEMs) that have their own ULPA fan-filter units at ISO 1-3","Killer defect sizes at N3/N2 are larger than 0.5 um, so ISO 5 air is clean enough for direct wafer exposure","The OHT vehicles carry wafers above the laminar flow zone, where particle counts are lowest"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The FOUP plus load port plus EFEM mini-environment means the wafer never sees ballroom air; only the small volumes that touch bare wafers must be ISO 1-3, which makes the ballroom layout, full automation, and human presence possible."},{"q":"A $20 billion fab has $15 billion in tools (depreciated over 5 years) and $5 billion in building (depreciated over 20 years) and produces 1.2 million wafers per year at full output. What is the approximate depreciation cost per wafer, and what happens to it at 50% loading?","options":["~$1,000 per wafer, unchanged at 50% loading because depreciation is a variable cost","~$2,700 per wafer, roughly doubling to ~$5,400 at 50% loading because depreciation is a fixed cost","~$16,700 per wafer, halving at 50% loading","~$270 per wafer, rising slightly at 50% loading"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Annual depreciation is $15B/5 + $5B/20 = $3.25B; divided by 1.2M wafers gives ~$2,700 per wafer. Depreciation accrues whether or not wafers are made, so at half the output the per-wafer charge doubles. This is why fabs run 24/7 at high utilization."},{"q":"According to Kingman's queueing approximation, what happens to the average waiting time at a tool as its utilization rises from 80% to 95%, all else equal?","options":["It rises by about 19% because utilization rose by 15 percentage points","It falls, because a busier tool processes lots faster","It rises by roughly a factor of 5, because the u/(1-u) term goes from 4 to 19","It stays constant as long as the tool is not the bottleneck"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Waiting time scales with u/(1-u): at 80% utilization this is 4, at 95% it is 19. This 'operating curve' is why bottleneck scanners are run at ~85-95% while other tools are deliberately given slack to protect cycle time."},{"q":"Why are copper seed PVD and copper electroplating tools placed close together and governed by a queue-time (Q-time) limit of a few hours?","options":["The plating bath must be kept at the same temperature as the PVD chamber","The 2-5 nm Cu seed layer oxidizes in air, and plating onto an oxidized seed produces voids and poor adhesion","The AMHS cannot transport FOUPs containing copper wafers more than a few hundred meters","Copper wafers must stay in vacuum, so the two tools share one cluster mainframe"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A freshly sputtered ultra-thin copper seed reacts with air; if the delay before plating is too long the plated film nucleates poorly and the interconnect is defective. Many step pairs in the flow carry similar Q-time limits for surface-chemistry reasons, and the MES dispatcher enforces them."},{"q":"A fab runs 100,000 wafers per month with 25 EUV layers. An NXE:3800E achieves an effective ~160 wafers per hour at production doses with ~85% availability. Approximately how many EUV scanners are required before adding margin?","options":["About 5","About 12","About 25","About 100"],"answer":2,"explanation":"2.5 million EUV exposures per month are needed. One scanner delivers 160 x 24 x 30.4 x 0.85 = ~99,000 exposures per month, so ~25 scanners are needed, and fabs add ~20% for dedication and ramp headroom, giving ~30 at ~$200M each."},{"q":"A fab draws 500 MW continuously and starts 100,000 wafers per month. Roughly how much electricity does each wafer account for?","options":["About 36 kWh","About 360 kWh","About 3,600 kWh","About 36,000 kWh"],"answer":2,"explanation":"500,000 kW x 24 h x 30.4 days is about 365 million kWh per month; divided by 100,000 wafers that is roughly 3,600 kWh per wafer, about a household's annual use. At $0.10 per kWh it is about $360 per wafer, 3-4% of a ~$10,000 wafer, consistent with the ~5% utilities line in the cost breakdown once water and gases are added."},{"q":"Why are FOUPs, load ports, and stockers in a leading-edge fab fitted with nitrogen purge?","options":["To cool the wafers quickly after high-temperature anneal and epitaxy steps","To displace oxygen and humidity so that native oxide regrowth on cleaned silicon and oxidation of copper and cobalt between BEOL steps are slowed, and so the FOUP's polymer walls do not release adsorbed HF or ammonia back onto the wafers","To keep the FOUP at positive pressure so that the OHT vehicle's gripper can hold it securely","To make the FOUP lighter so that the overhead hoist can lift it faster"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A purged FOUP holds wafers at a few percent humidity in a nearly oxygen-free atmosphere. That slows the surface chemistry that queue-time limits exist to control (native oxide on HF-last silicon, oxidation of the 2-5 nm copper seed, corrosion of exposed copper after CMP) and stops the polycarbonate body from feeding absorbed process chemicals back onto the wafers. Stockers and buffers purge lots that are waiting under humidity-sensitive Q-time limits for the same reason."}]},"6":{"file":"06-oxidation-and-deposition","questions":[{"q":"A 200 nm thermal oxide is grown on a bare silicon wafer. Roughly how far below the original silicon surface is the new Si/SiO2 interface, and why?","options":["0 nm: the oxide is deposited on top of the silicon and the interface stays at the original surface","About 88 nm: the oxidant diffuses through the oxide and reacts at the buried interface, and each SiO2 unit occupies about 2.27 times the volume of the Si atom it came from","About 200 nm: every nanometer of oxide replaces a nanometer of silicon one-for-one","About 44 nm: only the first fifth of the oxide consumes silicon, after which growth is purely additive"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Thermal oxidation consumes ~0.44 of the oxide thickness in silicon (Si density 5.0e22 cm^-3 vs SiO2 unit density 2.2e22 cm^-3), so 200 nm of oxide moves the interface ~88 nm down; the reaction happens at the buried interface, not the top surface."},{"q":"In the Deal-Grove model, why is wet (H2O) oxidation 20 to 25 times faster than dry O2 oxidation in the parabolic regime at 1,000 C?","options":["Water molecules are smaller and diffuse through SiO2 much faster than O2","The Si-Si bond-breaking reaction has a much lower activation energy with water","The solubility C* of H2O in SiO2 is roughly 600 times that of O2, so B = 2DC*/N1 is much larger even though D is similar or smaller","Steam is at higher pressure than oxygen in a furnace, so more molecules hit the surface"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The parabolic constant B scales with the oxidant solubility C* in the oxide; water's C* (~3e19 cm^-3) is ~600x oxygen's (~5e16 cm^-3), more than compensating for its roughly ten times lower diffusivity and the factor of two in N1."},{"q":"Using the Deal-Grove (111) constants at 1,000 C for dry O2 (A = 0.165 um, B = 0.0117 um^2/h, tau = 0.37 h), how long does it take to grow a 100 nm oxide?","options":["About 0.5 h, because 100 nm is well inside the linear regime where x = (B/A) t","About 1.9 h, from t = (x^2 + A x)/B - tau, in the mixed linear-parabolic regime since x is comparable to A","About 8.5 h, from the parabolic law t = x^2/B","About 14 h, because dry oxidation at 1,000 C is essentially diffusion-limited for any thickness"],"answer":1,"explanation":"t = (0.01 + 0.0165)/0.0117 - 0.37 = 2.26 - 0.37 = 1.9 h; with x = 100 nm smaller than A = 165 nm, the linear term dominates but neither limit alone is accurate."},{"q":"Why can a vertical LPCVD furnace deposit uniform polysilicon on 125 wafers stacked 6 mm apart, whereas epitaxy must be done one wafer at a time with an engineered showerhead or laminar flow?","options":["LPCVD runs in the surface-reaction-limited regime where rate depends on temperature, not gas flow, and low pressure makes the gas diffusivity huge; epi runs mass-transport limited where every wafer needs an identical boundary layer","Polysilicon is amorphous so it does not care about uniformity, while epitaxial films are crystalline","Silane is far more reactive than dichlorosilane so it can penetrate between closely stacked wafers","Furnaces run at higher temperature, which increases the sticking coefficient and improves step coverage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At low temperature and low pressure the surface reaction (Arrhenius, Ea ~1-2 eV) is the bottleneck and gas transport is not, so only temperature uniformity matters; at high temperature the rate is set by D/delta of the boundary layer, which requires controlled flow geometry over each wafer."},{"q":"In TMA/H2O ALD of Al2O3, the growth per cycle is about 0.1 nm while a monolayer of Al2O3 is about 0.3 nm. What explains the difference, and what does it imply about thickness control?","options":["The purge steps remove about two-thirds of the film each cycle; thickness must be corrected empirically","Bulky methyl ligands on adsorbed TMA sterically block neighboring OH sites, so each self-limiting cycle deposits only about one-third of a monolayer; thickness is approximately N cycles times a calibrated steady-state GPC after nucleation","Water desorbs part of the aluminum as volatile hydroxide; thicker films need higher water doses","Only one-third of the wafer surface is hydroxylated at any time; increasing TMA dose increases growth per cycle proportionally"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Saturation is reached when the surface is covered with methyl-terminated Al species that block adjacent sites, giving a sub-monolayer but perfectly repeatable GPC; because each half-reaction is self-limiting, increasing the dose does not change the thickness."},{"q":"Why does the CVD tungsten process for a contact plug begin with a Ti/TiN liner and a thin WF6 + SiH4 (or B2H6) nucleation layer before the WF6 + H2 bulk deposition?","options":["The nucleation layer sets the crystal orientation so the bulk tungsten grows epitaxially with low resistivity","Hydrogen reduction of WF6 only works above 600 C, so the nucleation layer must be deposited first and used to heat the wafer","WF6 does not nucleate on dielectrics and attacks bare silicon (forming wormholes and SiF4) while its HF byproduct etches oxide, so a TiN fluorine barrier and a silane-reduced seed layer are needed before H2 reduction can take over","The Ti/TiN liner is a copper diffusion barrier required by the later dual-damascene steps"],"answer":2,"explanation":"WF6 reacts directly with silicon (2 WF6 + 3 Si -> 2 W + 3 SiF4) and will not reduce on SiO2 with H2 alone; the Ti/TiN glue-and-barrier layer plus the SiH4-reduced nucleation layer protect the substrate and provide a surface for the fast, conformal H2 bulk reaction at 400-450 C."},{"q":"Why does conventional magnetron sputtering fail to coat the bottom and lower sidewalls of a 20 nm wide, 60 nm deep via, and how does ionized PVD fix it?","options":["Sputtered atoms carry a positive charge and are repelled by the insulating sidewalls; ionized PVD neutralizes them before they reach the wafer","Sputtered atoms fly in straight lines and arrive from a wide range of angles, so the top corners grow an overhang that shadows the bottom; ionized PVD ionizes most of the metal flux in flight and a negative wafer bias pulls the ions in at normal incidence, so they reach the via bottom and can resputter it onto the lower sidewalls","The argon pressure is too high for any atom to cross the target-to-wafer gap; ionized PVD simply lowers the pressure below 1 mtorr","Sputtered metal condenses only on heated surfaces and the via bottom is the coldest spot; ionized PVD heats the wafer so the bottom coats first"],"answer":1,"explanation":"PVD is line-of-sight: atoms leave the target with a roughly cosine angular spread and land on whatever faces them, so the via mouth and upper sidewalls collect material while the bottom is shadowed. Ionizing 50-90% of the metal flux (RF coil or self-ionized plasma) and biasing the wafer makes the arrival direction normal to the wafer, which reaches the bottom and, at higher bias, resputters bottom material onto the sidewalls where continuity matters."},{"q":"Selective source/drain epitaxy grows SiGe:B in the recessed silicon cavities beside the gate but leaves the neighbouring oxide and nitride bare. What makes the growth selective?","options":["The dielectric surfaces are covered with photoresist during the epi step, which is stripped afterwards","Germane and dichlorosilane react only with silicon atoms, so no film can ever form on an oxide","Silicon nucleates immediately on crystalline silicon but only after an incubation delay on SiO2 and SiN, as isolated nuclei; HCl in the gas etches those nuclei, and at the right HCl:Si balance the net growth rate on dielectric is zero while growth on silicon stays positive","The growth temperature is set so that the precursors decompose on silicon but are below their decomposition temperature on oxide"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Selectivity comes from the HCl balance, not from masking: the small amorphous nuclei that eventually form on SiO2 and SiN are etched away by HCl faster than they grow, while the crystalline film on exposed silicon grows faster than it is etched. Chlorine-containing precursors (DCS) need less HCl, and cyclic deposition-etch (CDE) alternates fast non-selective growth with short HCl or Cl2 etches to strip the dielectric nuclei. Photoresist cannot survive the 550-700 C growth temperature."}]},"7":{"file":"07-lithography-duv","questions":[{"q":"An ASML NXT:2100i (193.4 nm, NA 1.35) is run at a production k1 of 0.28. What is the approximate single-exposure half-pitch, and what happens to it at the theoretical k1 floor?","options":["~20 nm; at k1 = 0.25 it reaches ~10 nm, which is why SAQP was abandoned","~134 nm, because the effective wavelength in water is 193/1.44","~40 nm (80 nm pitch); at k1 = 0.25 it reaches ~36 nm, so ~72–80 nm is the tightest pitch 193i can print in one exposure","~72 nm; the k1 floor is 0.5 because only the 0th and +1st diffraction orders can ever enter the lens"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CD = k1·λ/NA = 0.28 × 193.4 / 1.35 ≈ 40 nm half-pitch. Two-beam imaging with off-axis illumination allows k1 down to 0.25 (≈ 36 nm), which is why 193i single-exposure pitches bottom out around 76–80 nm and tighter pitches need multi-patterning."},{"q":"In a chemically amplified resist, why is the post-exposure bake (PEB) rather than the exposure itself the step that determines the final line width?","options":["The PEB evaporates the casting solvent, which is what makes exposed resist soluble in TMAH","Exposure only converts PAG molecules into a sparse latent image of acid; during PEB each acid molecule catalytically cleaves hundreds to thousands of protecting groups (t-BOC or tertiary esters) and diffuses 5–20 nm, so PEB temperature, time, and delay set both the chemical gain and the edge blur","The PEB cross-links the novolac resin so it survives the plasma etch","The PEB is where the DNQ undergoes the Wolff rearrangement to form indene carboxylic acid"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In a CAR the photon creates only an acid; deprotection happens thermally during PEB with the acid regenerated after every reaction (gain ~10^2–10^3). Acid diffusion during PEB is also the main resolution/LER/sensitivity trade-off knob, which is why PEB is controlled to ~0.1 °C and the exposure-to-PEB delay to seconds."},{"q":"Why did water immersion at 193 nm win over the 157 nm F2 excimer as the path beyond dry ArF?","options":["157 nm needed CaF2 for every lens element (intrinsic birefringence, boule supply), had no workable pellicle, and needed fluorinated resists with poor etch resistance, while water (n = 1.44, absorbing well under 1% in a 1 mm film) raised the NA from 0.93 to 1.35 using existing lens materials, masks, and resists, giving a larger resolution gain than the 193→157 wavelength step","Water absorbs strongly at 157 nm, so immersion was only possible at 193 nm; the industry chose immersion because it made resists cheaper","The F2 laser could not reach 6 kHz repetition rate, limiting throughput below 100 wph","157 nm light is EUV and requires vacuum and reflective optics"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The 157 nm programs were killed ~2003 by materials problems (CaF2 birefringence, no pellicle, poor resists). Immersion raises NA = n·sin θ to 1.35 with water, an effective wavelength of 134 nm, using the fused-silica lenses, 6% MoSi masks, and CAR platforms already in place."},{"q":"In self-aligned double patterning (SADP), what physically sets the width of the final lines, and what is the main design consequence of the process?","options":["The lithographic mandrel CD sets the line width; the consequence is that overlay between the two exposures adds ~3–5 nm of space variation","The developer normality (0.26 N TMAH) sets the line width; the consequence is that only positive-tone resists can be used","The etch trim sets the line width; the consequence is that pitch cannot be reduced below the mandrel pitch","The ALD spacer thickness deposited on the mandrel sidewalls sets the line width (controllable to < 0.5 nm); because each spacer wraps around its mandrel, every line is a closed loop and line-ends must be created with a separate cut mask, which restricts the layer to 1D gridded lines at one pitch"],"answer":3,"explanation":"SADP prints mandrels at the relaxed pitch, deposits a conformal ALD spacer, etches it back, and pulls the mandrel; the two spacers per mandrel halve the pitch (80 → 40 nm) with CD set by deposition. The loops and single-pitch constraint are why cut masks and restricted 1D layouts appear at 20 nm and below; LELE, not SADP, is the scheme whose spaces depend on overlay."},{"q":"Depth of focus scales as k2·λ/NA². For the NA 1.35 immersion lens with k2 ≈ 0.6, what is the approximate total DOF, and which fab practices follow directly from it?","options":["~600 nm; resist films can be 1–2 µm thick and wafer leveling is unimportant","~60–65 nm; resists are only ~80–120 nm thick, spin-on-carbon/SiARC tri-layers planarize topography, and the scanner maps wafer height field by field with a level sensor on the second TWINSCAN stage","~6 nm; this is why 193i can only be used on perfectly flat bare-silicon wafers","~134 nm; this equals the effective wavelength in water"],"answer":1,"explanation":"DOF = 0.6 × 193.4 / 1.35² ≈ 64 nm total (±32 nm), the entire budget for wafer flatness, topography, chuck particles, leveling, and lens field curvature. Thin resists, planarizing underlayers, and per-field leveling on the measurement stage are the consequences."},{"q":"Which statement about EUV versus DUV layer usage at TSMC N5/N3-class nodes and the litho tool market is correct?","options":["EUV replaced all 193i layers at N5; ASML's DUV business has since collapsed to a few percent of revenue","N3 uses EUV for all ~80 layers, which is why the mask count fell for the first time","N5 uses ~14 EUV layers and N3 ~20–25 out of roughly 80 mask layers, each EUV layer replacing several 193i multi-patterning exposures, so the majority of layers remain 193i/KrF/i-line; ASML holds ~90% of the litho market with DUV still about half its system revenue and China a large DUV customer","Nikon and Canon together hold about half the ArF immersion market, with ASML's dominance limited to EUV"],"answer":2,"explanation":"EUV took the ~15–25 tightest layers (fins, gates, contacts, M1–M4, cuts) where SAQP/LELELE were prohibitively expensive; layers that print in one 193i pass stay on DUV because they are cheaper. N5 was the first TSMC node with fewer masks than its predecessor. ASML is ~90% of litho by revenue; Nikon and Canon are a few percent each."},{"q":"A layer is qualified at \"8% exposure latitude at 64 nm depth of focus\". What does that statement mean, and which errors consume the budget it describes?","options":["The laser must deliver exactly 8% of its rated power and the lens must be refocused every 80 nm of scan travel; the budget is consumed by stage speed and reticle heating","The dose may vary by ±4% and the focus by ±32 nm, in any combination, with the printed CD staying within ±10% of target; dose-equivalent errors (laser ~±0.3%, slit uniformity ~±0.3%, resist-thickness swing ~±1%, PEB temperature ~±0.4 nm of CD via ~2 nm/°C, i.e. ~±0.4% dose-equivalent at ~1 nm per 1% dose) and focus errors (levelling ~±15 nm, wafer flatness and topography ~±20 nm, chuck ~±10 nm, lens heating ~±10 nm, ~±29 nm in quadrature against the ±32 nm half-range) each eat into it, and the layer is manufacturable only if what remains is positive","8% of dies fail at the wafer edge because the immersion hood leaves 80 nm of water behind; the budget is consumed by watermarks","The resist absorbs 8% of the light in an 80 nm film; the budget is consumed by photon shot noise"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The process window is read from a focus-exposure matrix and its Bossung plot: exposure latitude is the full dose range (here ±4%) and DOF the full focus range (here ±40 nm) over which CD stays within ±10% and sidewall angle stays in spec. Every source of dose and focus error, added in quadrature or worst-case, is subtracted from that rectangle, which is why resists are ~100 nm, underlayers planarize, backsides are rinsed and the level sensor maps every field."},{"q":"At a 30 mJ/cm² ArF dose, why is a line-edge roughness of ~3–5 nm 3σ described as a statistical floor rather than an engineering defect, and what does the RLS scaling say it would cost to halve it?","options":["Because the 193 nm laser's 0.3 pm bandwidth produces a 3–5 nm chromatic blur; halving LER requires a 0.15 pm laser","Because the developer normality (0.26 N) sets a 3–5 nm dissolution front; halving LER needs a double puddle","Because 30 mJ/cm² is ~2.9 × 10¹⁶ photons/cm², of which a 100 nm film absorbs only ~10%, giving ~10¹⁹–10²⁰ acids/cm³, so a 20 × 20 × 100 nm line segment holds only ~400 acids and its count fluctuates by 1/√400 ≈ 5% (shot noise); with LER² × dose × resolution³ ≈ constant, halving LER at fixed resolution costs ~4× the dose","Because the ALD spacer in SADP is only controllable to 0.5 nm; halving LER needs a thicker spacer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Roughness is seeded by counting statistics: a 6.4 eV photon carries 1.03 × 10⁻¹⁸ J, so 30 mJ/cm² is ~2.9 × 10¹⁶ photons/cm²; after ~10% absorption and imperfect acid generation the film holds ~10¹⁹–10²⁰ acids/cm³, one per 2–5 nm cube, and a 4 × 10⁻¹⁷ cm³ line segment contains only ~400, fluctuating by √400 = 20 (~5%). The resolution/LER/sensitivity triangle then says smoother edges at the same resolution need quadratically more photons, which is the same trade-off that limits EUV throughput."}]},"8":{"file":"08-euv-lithography","questions":[{"q":"Why is EUV lithography done at 13.5 nm rather than some other short wavelength?","options":["13.5 nm is the shortest wavelength any laser can produce at useful power","Mo/Si multilayer Bragg mirrors have their reflectance peak (~67-70%) near 13.5 nm and highly charged tin ions emit strongly in that band","Fused silica lenses become transparent again below 14 nm","13.5 nm is exactly one fourteenth of 193 nm, which keeps the 4x reduction optics compatible with DUV reticles"],"answer":1,"explanation":"No material transmits EUV, so the optics must be reflective; the only high-reflectance normal-incidence mirror is a Mo/Si multilayer whose Bragg peak sits near 13.5 nm, and the Sn8+ to Sn14+ unresolved transition array happens to radiate efficiently there."},{"q":"An NXE source has 11 reflections between the intermediate focus and the wafer, each at about 67% reflectance. Roughly what fraction of the in-band light at the IF reaches the wafer?","options":["About 67%, since only the weakest mirror matters","About 30%","About 1-2%","About 0.01%"],"answer":2,"explanation":"0.67 raised to the 11th power is about 0.012, so only ~1-2% of the light survives (less with a pellicle), which is why a 500 W source delivers only a few watts to the wafer and why every mirror is counted."},{"q":"What is the purpose of the pre-pulse in the LPP source?","options":["It ionizes the tin to Sn8+ so the main pulse only has to heat it","It flattens the ~27 um droplet into a disk or mist a few hundred micrometers across, so the CO2 main pulse is absorbed efficiently instead of reflecting off a small dense sphere","It cleans tin off the collector between main pulses","It synchronizes the droplet generator's piezo actuator with the laser"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The droplet is smaller than the 10.6 um focal spot and acts as a mirror; the low-energy pre-pulse expands it into a low-density, spot-sized target 1-3 us before the main pulse, raising conversion efficiency from ~2% to ~5-6%."},{"q":"A 13.5 nm photon carries ~92 eV. At a dose of 30 mJ/cm2, roughly how many photons are incident per square nanometer of resist, and why does this matter?","options":["About 20 photons/nm2; the small count makes photon shot noise a first-order cause of stochastic defects such as missing contacts","About 2,000 photons/nm2; the high count makes EUV resists heat up and outgas","About 290 photons/nm2, the same as ArF, so stochastics are no worse than DUV","About 0.2 photons/nm2, so most features receive no photons at all"],"answer":0,"explanation":"30 mJ/cm2 is 3e-16 J/nm2 and one photon is 1.47e-17 J, giving ~20 photons/nm2 incident (only ~3 absorbed in a 30 nm CAR); a 16 nm edge pixel is decided by a few hundred photons, so Poisson noise of several percent is unavoidable."},{"q":"Why are the High-NA (0.55) EXE projection optics anamorphic, with 8x reduction in one direction and 4x in the other?","options":["To double throughput by exposing two fields at once","Because Zeiss could not polish a rotationally symmetric mirror larger than 500 mm","Because at 4x and NA 0.55 the reticle-side cone (about +/- 8 degrees) would exceed the 6 degree chief ray angle, so the reflected and incident beams would overlap and absorber shadowing would be intolerable; 8x in the scan direction fixes this while 4x in the other keeps a usable 26 x 16.5 mm half-field","Because EUV reticles are rectangular rather than square"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Reticle-side NA is wafer NA divided by reduction; halving it in one direction only solves the angular overlap and mask 3D problem without quartering the field, at the cost of a half-field that forces stitching or chiplet splits for reticle-limited dies."},{"q":"Why is the source vessel back-filled with hydrogen at roughly 1 mbar instead of being kept at the hardest vacuum possible?","options":["Hydrogen cools the CO2 laser amplifiers","Hydrogen raises the plasma's conversion efficiency by adding electrons","Hydrogen slows tin ions before they sputter the collector, and hydrogen radicals convert deposited tin into gaseous stannane (SnH4) that the pumps remove, while absorbing very little 13.5 nm light","Hydrogen prevents the tin droplets from freezing in flight"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Debris mitigation is the central reliability problem of the LPP source; H2 is the least EUV-absorbing gas, thermalizes the fast ions, and chemically etches tin off the collector as SnH4, extending collector life from weeks to many months."},{"q":"At fixed wavelength and process factor, increasing numerical aperture from 0.33 to 0.55 changes the characteristic resolution by what factor?","options":["It becomes 0.33/0.55 ≈ 0.60 of its previous value","It becomes 0.55/0.33 ≈ 1.67 times larger","It is unchanged","It improves by exactly 14 times"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Resolution scales inversely with numerical aperture. Smaller features are possible, but depth of focus also shrinks and other process constraints remain."},{"q":"Why should two EUV throughput specifications be compared at the same exposure dose?","options":["Dose determines wafer diameter","A higher required dose generally increases the light delivery time, so different-dose throughput figures are not directly comparable","Dose has no influence on exposure","All tools run at identical throughput regardless of resist"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The resist needs a certain energy per area. More dose demands more exposure time unless source power or another part of the system compensates; overhead also matters."}]},"9":{"file":"09-etch","questions":[{"q":"In Coburn and Winters' 1979 experiment, silicon exposed to XeF2 alone etched at ~5 Å/min and to a 450 eV Ar+ beam alone at ~2 Å/min. What happened when both were applied simultaneously, and what does it imply about the roles of ions and radicals in plasma etching?","options":["The rate was ~7 Å/min (the sum), showing ions and radicals act independently","The rate rose to ~55 Å/min, far above the sum, showing ions enable the surface chemistry to proceed rapidly wherever they land while radicals supply the reactant","The rate dropped below 2 Å/min because ions sputtered away the adsorbed fluorine before it could react","The rate was unchanged at ~5 Å/min because fluorine chemistry is already at its limit"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The combined rate (~55 Å/min) was roughly eight times the sum of the individual rates. This ion-enhanced synergy is the basis of anisotropic plasma etching: chemistry supplies rate and selectivity, ions supply direction by activating the reaction only on surfaces they strike."},{"q":"Why does a dual-frequency capacitively coupled dielectric etcher apply ~60 MHz and ~2 MHz (or 400 kHz) to the electrodes at the same time?","options":["The two frequencies beat together to produce a resonance that heats the wafer uniformly","High frequency is used for the mask open and low frequency for the main etch, switched sequentially","Ions cannot follow fields above their plasma frequency (a few MHz), so the high frequency controls plasma density (ion flux) while the low frequency sets the sheath voltage (ion energy), letting the two be tuned independently","The 2 MHz supply is only for endpoint detection through RF impedance monitoring"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Electrons respond to all RF frequencies but ions respond only below their plasma frequency (~3 MHz at 10^10 cm^-3). The high-frequency source therefore generates density with little added ion energy, and the low-frequency bias develops a large sheath voltage that ions follow, decoupling flux from energy."},{"q":"In a fluorocarbon (e.g., C4F6/Ar/O2) contact-hole etch, what mechanism makes the etch proceed through SiO2 but stop on silicon or silicon nitride?","options":["Fluorine radicals react spontaneously with SiO2 but are inert toward Si and Si3N4","Oxygen released from the etched SiO2 consumes the deposited CFx polymer as CO/CO2/COF2, keeping the film thin, whereas on Si or nitride the polymer thickens until ions can no longer penetrate it","Silicon and nitride have higher sputter thresholds than SiO2 so the ions cannot remove them","The wafer is cooled below the boiling point of SiF4 when the etch reaches silicon"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fluorocarbon selectivity is a polymer-thickness effect: the oxygen in oxide burns the carbon film so etching continues, while oxygen-free substrates accumulate a thick blocking polymer. Tuning the gas F/C ratio (or adding O2/H2) moves the balance between etching and deposition."},{"q":"A 3D NAND channel-hole etch removes 10 µm of an ONON stack. Using a conventional rate of ~100–150 nm/min, roughly how long is the single-wafer etch, and why does cryogenic etch (Lam Cryo 3.0, TEL at −60 to −70 °C) matter so much economically?","options":["About 5 minutes; cryogenic etch mainly improves selectivity to the carbon hardmask","About 60–100 minutes per wafer for one step; a ~2.5x rate gain from HF-based cryogenic chemistry cuts the hundreds of channel-hole chambers a NAND fab needs by roughly half","About 10 seconds; cryogenic etch only exists to reduce greenhouse-gas emissions","About 8 hours; cryogenic etch is needed because room-temperature plasmas cannot ignite in HF gas"],"answer":1,"explanation":"At 100–150 nm/min a 10 µm hole takes over an hour, so chamber throughput is ~1 wafer/hour and a 100k-wafer/month fab needs on the order of hundreds of channel-hole chambers. TEL reports 10 µm in 33 minutes and Lam claims 2.5x conventional rate, which directly translates to fewer $3–6M chambers."},{"q":"In Cl2/Ar atomic layer etching of silicon, why is the Ar+ ion energy restricted to a window of roughly 40–70 eV?","options":["Below 40 eV argon cannot be ionized; above 70 eV the chamber walls sputter","It must be above the threshold to desorb the weakly bound chlorinated SiClx surface layer but below the sputter threshold of bare silicon, so each cycle removes only the chlorinated layer and then self-limits","The chlorine adsorption step only saturates at that ion energy","It matches the energy needed to break the Cl–Cl bond in the gas phase"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Self-limiting removal requires that ions remove the modified (chlorinated) layer but not the unmodified substrate. Inside the ALE window the etch per cycle (~0.5–1 Å for Si) is independent of ion dose and local flux, eliminating ARDE and microloading."},{"q":"A film 20 nm thick is etched isotropically (wet etch) through a 20 nm wide mask opening. What is the approximate width of the finished feature at the film's top surface, and what does this illustrate?","options":["20 nm; wet etch transfers the mask faithfully if the bath is fresh","30 nm; the undercut is half the film thickness on each side","60 nm; isotropic etching undercuts the mask by the full film thickness on each side, so wet etch cannot pattern features smaller than about twice the film thickness","10 nm; surface tension in the liquid shrinks the opening"],"answer":2,"explanation":"An isotropic etch proceeds sideways under the mask by the same distance it goes down, so a 20 nm film adds ~20 nm of undercut on each side: 20 + 2 × 20 = 60 nm. This geometric limit is why patterning moved to directional plasma etch while wet etch survives for blanket strips and deliberate undercuts like nanosheet release."},{"q":"A 20 nm protected line sits over a 20 nm film. In the simple isotropic model, what happens as the film is etched through?","options":["The protected line becomes a 60 nm gate","Lateral undercut can meet beneath the line and destroy it","The mask grows 20 nm thicker","Only the vertical dimension changes"],"answer":1,"explanation":"An opening widens; a protected line narrows. With about 20 nm lateral reach from each side, a 20 nm line cannot survive in this idealized geometry."},{"q":"An etch removes 100 nm of target film at a target-to-mask selectivity of 20:1. What nominal mask thickness does that consume?","options":["0.2 nm","5 nm","20 nm","2,000 nm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"100/20 = 5 nm. The starting mask must additionally cover nonuniformity, over-etch and corner erosion."}]},"10":{"file":"10-doping","questions":[{"q":"A p+ extension is implanted with 11B+ at 10 keV (Rp ~ 37 nm, delta-Rp ~ 17 nm) to a dose of 1e15 cm^-2. Roughly what is the as-implanted peak boron concentration?","options":["~ 6e17 cm^-3","~ 2e19 cm^-3","~ 2e20 cm^-3","~ 6e22 cm^-3"],"answer":2,"explanation":"For a Gaussian profile the peak is Np = Q / (sqrt(2*pi) * delta-Rp) = 0.4 * 1e15 / 1.7e-6 cm = 2.4e20 cm^-3, which is right at boron's solid-solubility limit; this is why 1e15 cm^-2 is the canonical high dose."},{"q":"Why does a 1,050 C spike anneal lasting under a second often produce LESS boron diffusion than an 800 C anneal for 10 minutes?","options":["Boron diffusivity is lower at 1,050 C than at 800 C","Transient enhanced diffusion from dissolving {311} interstitial defects is worse at lower temperature because the interstitials are released slowly instead of annihilating quickly at the surface","The spike anneal melts the surface so dopants cannot move","At 800 C boron pairs with vacancies and becomes highly mobile"],"answer":1,"explanation":"TED is driven by the excess silicon interstitials left by the implant; at 700-900 C the {311} defects release them slowly over minutes and boron diffusivity is enhanced 1,000x, while a spike anneal gets through the transient in under a second."},{"q":"A high-current implanter runs in deceleration mode: ions are extracted at 5 keV and slowed to 500 eV before the wafer. What is the specific risk this creates and how do modern tools mitigate it?","options":["Space charge blows up the beam; mitigated by a plasma flood gun","The Faraday cup over-counts dose; mitigated by a suppression electrode","Channeling increases; mitigated by 7 degree tilt","Energy contamination: ions neutralised by charge exchange before the decel stage arrive at the full 5 keV and land ~ 10x deeper; mitigated by a bend or energy filter after deceleration"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A neutral cannot be decelerated by the electrostatic decel stage, so a few percent of the beam arriving at 10x the intended energy creates a deep tail that sets the junction depth; placing the energy filter after the decel stage removes anything not at the final energy."},{"q":"Which statement about the analyser magnet is correct? A 49 amu BF2+ ion at 30 keV is selected instead of 11B+ because...","options":["BF2+ has a higher charge state so it gains more energy per volt","BF2+ delivers its boron atom at only about 30 x (11/49) = 6.7 keV, giving a shallower profile than the tool could otherwise reach, and the heavy fragments amorphise the surface as they go","BF2+ is the only boron-bearing ion produced in a BF3 plasma","Fluorine acts as a donor and compensates the boron"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The molecular ion shares its kinetic energy among its atoms in proportion to mass, so a 30 keV BF2+ implant is effectively a 6.7 keV boron implant with a self-amorphising fluorine co-implant; the same principle is used by B18H22 cluster ions."},{"q":"Why are the channels of modern FinFET and nanosheet transistors left undoped, with threshold voltage set by gate work-function metals rather than a channel implant?","options":["At 1e18 cm^-3 a 20 nm x 20 nm x 20 nm channel contains only ~ 8 dopant atoms, so Poisson statistics (random dopant fluctuation) would make the threshold voltage uncontrollable","Implanters cannot reach the 1e17-1e18 cm^-3 doping range","Dopants in the channel would diffuse into the gate oxide during the anneal","The high-k dielectric is destroyed by any implant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"1e18 cm^-3 x (20 nm)^3 = 8 atoms, with a +/-35 % statistical spread; fully depleted fins and sheets do not need channel doping for electrostatics, so the industry moved V_T control to TiN/TiAl/dipole layers in the metal gate."},{"q":"Silicon carbide power MOSFETs are implanted with aluminium at ~ 500 C wafer temperature and annealed at 1,600-1,800 C under a carbon cap. Why is each of these three unusual conditions necessary?","options":["Hot implant increases beam current; the high anneal temperature drives the Al deep; the carbon cap adds n-type dopant","Hot implant reduces channeling; the high temperature dissolves {311} defects; the carbon cap acts as an anti-reflective layer","Hot implant prevents amorphisation (amorphous SiC does not regrow in the correct polytype); ~ 1,700 C is needed to activate Al because dopants barely diffuse or activate in SiC below that; the carbon cap stops the SiC surface subliming and step-bunching","All three are only needed because SiC wafers are 150 mm rather than 300 mm"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Diffusion in SiC is negligible, so every doped region must be implanted to its final shape and activated at extreme temperature; amorphised SiC recrystallises as defective 3C material, and bare SiC loses silicon by sublimation above ~ 1,500 C, hence the graphitised-photoresist cap."},{"q":"Boron's equilibrium diffusivity at 800 C is about 4e-17 cm^2/s, so a 10-minute anneal should give a wander distance sqrt(Dt) of only ~1.6 nm. Yet a 1e14 cm^-2 boron implant annealed at 800 C for 10 minutes moves ~50 nm. Roughly what enhancement of the diffusivity does this imply, and what causes it?","options":["~30x, caused by the surface oxide injecting vacancies","~1,000x, caused by the excess silicon interstitials released from dissolving {311} defects (transient enhanced diffusion)","~30,000x, caused by boron melting the surface","~1,000,000x, caused by channeling during the implant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The wander distance scales as the square root of D, so moving 32x further (50 nm / 1.6 nm) needs about 32^2 = 1,000x the diffusivity. That is the interstitial supersaturation of 1e3-1e4 created when the implant's excess interstitials, stored in {311} defects, dissolve over minutes at 700-950 C."},{"q":"A four-point probe on the monitor wafer from a 5e14 cm^-2 arsenic source/drain implant reads V/I = 61.8 ohm after the spike anneal. Full activation at ~1e20 cm^-3 (mobility ~90 cm^2/V.s) would give ~140 ohm/sq. What does the reading tell the process engineer? For this diagnostic estimate, assume the carrier mobility and profile shape match the reference.","options":["R_s = 4.532 x 61.8 = 280 ohm/sq, i.e. only ~50 % of the dose is active; suspect a cold anneal or dopant clustering","R_s = 61.8 ohm/sq, so the implant was over-dosed by about 2x","R_s = 280 ohm/sq, so the junction is twice as deep as intended","The probe cannot read arsenic layers; a SIMS profile is required"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sheet resistance is R_s = (pi/ln 2) x V/I = 4.532 x 61.8 = 280 ohm/sq, twice the fully activated value. Since R_s = 1/(q x Q_active x mobility), twice the resistance means half the active dose (about 50 % activation); if the pre-anneal ThermaProbe reading was normal, the implanter delivered the dose and the anneal is the suspect. In production, mobility and profile changes can also change sheet resistance, so this measurement alone does not uniquely determine activation."}]},"11":{"file":"11-transistor-architectures","questions":[{"q":"A production FinFET has a subthreshold swing of 65 mV/dec. If a designer lowers the threshold voltage of a cell by 130 mV to gain speed, roughly how does its off-state leakage change, and why can no conventional MOSFET at 300 K do better than about 60 mV/dec?","options":["Leakage doubles; the limit comes from the mobility of silicon","Leakage rises ~100x; the limit is (kT/q) ln(10) times the body factor, set by the Boltzmann distribution of carriers in the source","Leakage rises ~10x; the limit is set by gate-oxide tunneling","Leakage is unchanged; only drain-induced barrier lowering changes I_off"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Each 65 mV of V_T reduction multiplies I_off by 10, so 130 mV gives ~100x. SS = (kT/q) ln(10) x (1 + C_dep/C_ox) >= 59.6 mV/dec at 300 K because carriers surmounting the source barrier follow a Boltzmann distribution."},{"q":"Using the double-gate scale-length approximation lambda = sqrt((eps_Si/(2 eps_ox)) x t_Si x t_ox) with eps_Si/eps_ox = 3, a 7 nm fin, and 0.9 nm EOT, what minimum gate length does the L_g >= 5 lambda rule give, and what is the main way a nanosheet lowers it further?","options":["~ 8 nm; by using a higher-k dielectric","~ 45 nm; by making the fin taller","~ 15 nm; by gating a thinner (5-6 nm) body on all four sides, which reduces lambda to ~ 2 nm","~ 15 nm; by increasing channel doping to shrink the depletion depth"],"answer":2,"explanation":"lambda = sqrt(1.5 x 7 x 0.9) ~ 3.1 nm, so L_g >= ~ 15 nm. A nanosheet is thinner and gated on four sides, giving lambda ~ 1.9 nm and allowing L_g ~ 10-12 nm; doping the channel would degrade mobility and add variability."},{"q":"In a replacement-metal-gate (gate-last) flow, why is the polysilicon gate deposited first and then removed, rather than depositing the HfO2/metal stack at the start?","options":["Polysilicon is easier to pattern with EUV than metal","The high-k and work-function metals cannot survive the ~1000 C source/drain activation anneal without work-function drift and interfacial-layer regrowth, so the real gate is built after all high-temperature steps","Metal gates require a self-aligned contact cap, which can only be formed on polysilicon","The dummy poly provides the tensile strain needed for NMOS mobility"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Gate-first flows (IBM/Samsung 32/28 nm) exposed the HKMG stack to the S/D anneal and lost V_T control; gate-last uses sacrificial poly to define the device, does the anneal, then replaces poly with IL/HfO2/WFM/fill in the empty trench."},{"q":"A standard-cell library on a FinFET node has CPP = 51 nm, minimum metal pitch = 30 nm, and 6-track cells. Estimate the NAND2-based transistor density.","options":["~ 45 MTr/mm2","~ 91 MTr/mm2","~ 145 MTr/mm2","~ 290 MTr/mm2"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cell height = 6 x 30 = 180 nm; a NAND2 is 3 CPP = 153 nm wide; area = 153 x 180 = 27,540 nm2 = 0.02754 um2 holding 4 transistors, so 4 / 0.02754 = ~ 145 MTr/mm2 (TSMC N5 class)."},{"q":"Which step is unique to the gate-all-around nanosheet flow and why is it considered the hardest?","options":["Fin reveal by STI recess, because the etch depth sets fin height","Inner spacer formation: the SiGe layers are laterally recessed 5-8 nm, a dielectric is ALD-filled into the cavities and etched back, and the recess must be identical for every sheet because it sets each sheet's gate length and gate-to-S/D isolation","Self-aligned contact cap formation, because the gate recess must be uniform","Dual stress liner deposition, because tensile and compressive films must be patterned separately"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Without inner spacers the replacement gate metal filling the inter-sheet gaps would short to the source/drain epi; the lateral recess suffers loading differences between top and bottom sheets, and 1 nm of non-uniformity becomes 1 nm of L_g mismatch."},{"q":"Intel's PowerVia and TSMC's A16 Super Power Rail both deliver power from the back of the wafer. What is the key structural difference between them, and what shared process cost do both incur?","options":["PowerVia uses copper while SPR uses ruthenium; both require High-NA EUV","PowerVia uses nano-TSVs from the M0 rails down through the STI, while SPR makes backside contacts directly to the source/drain epi; both require bonding to a carrier wafer and thinning the device wafer to a few hundred nanometers","PowerVia is a sequential CFET while SPR is monolithic; both require a Si/SiGe superlattice","PowerVia places signals on the back and power on the front, the reverse of SPR; both eliminate the need for CMP"],"answer":1,"explanation":"SPR's direct S/D contact is the most area-efficient variant (7-10% density gain over N2P) but the hardest to land; both approaches need carrier bonding, extreme backside thinning, backside litho aligned to front-side marks, and accept a worse thermal path."},{"q":"A gate stack is physically 2.4 nm thick but has an equivalent oxide thickness (EOT) of about 0.95 nm. How can both statements be correct?","options":["EOT is the physical thickness after polishing","The high-k layers provide the capacitance of a thinner SiO2 layer while retaining a thicker physical barrier","The oxide expands when electrical power is removed","EOT includes only the metal gate"],"answer":1,"explanation":"EOT compares electrical capacitance with SiO2. It is not the microscope-measured thickness of the multilayer insulator; high-k materials provide stronger capacitive coupling per unit thickness."},{"q":"Logic density improves substantially on a new node while SRAM cell area changes little. What follows for a cache-heavy chip?","options":["Its total area must shrink by exactly the logic density gain","Its total area can shrink less than a logic-only estimate predicts","SRAM contains no transistors and is unaffected by manufacturing","Its cache must become nonvolatile"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A chip combines logic, SRAM, analog and interfaces. Each fraction scales differently, so a logic-library density claim does not determine the complete die area."}]},"12":{"file":"12-interconnect-beol","questions":[{"q":"A copper line is scaled from 40 nm wide to 20 nm wide. Beyond the geometric 1/(w x h) increase in resistance, why does its effective resistivity also rise several-fold?","options":["Copper oxidizes more readily in narrow trenches, raising its resistivity","The line width is below copper's ~39 nm electron mean free path, so surface and grain-boundary scattering add to phonon scattering, while the fixed ~3 to 4 nm barrier/liner per sidewall takes a growing share of the cross section","The low-k dielectric around the line becomes conductive at small spacings","Electroplated copper has a different crystal structure below 30 nm"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fuchs-Sondheimer surface scattering and Mayadas-Shatzkes grain-boundary scattering raise effective resistivity to ~5 to 8 uOhm-cm at 20 nm width, and the non-scaling TaN/Co barrier and liner consume roughly a third of the width."},{"q":"Why is copper interconnect built by damascene (etch dielectric, fill metal, polish) rather than by the subtractive etch used for aluminum?","options":["Copper is too soft to survive plasma etching","Copper halide etch products (CuCl, CuCl2) are not volatile below roughly 200 to 250 C, which photoresist cannot survive, and copper must be sealed by a diffusion barrier on every side","Damascene is cheaper because it needs fewer lithography steps","Copper cannot be sputtered as a blanket film"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Aluminum chlorides are volatile at ~180 C, but copper chlorides are not at resist-compatible temperatures, so the pattern is etched into the dielectric and copper is inlaid and polished back."},{"q":"In copper electrochemical deposition, what mechanism causes a narrow via to fill from the bottom up (superfill) instead of pinching off at the top?","options":["The electric field is strongest at the via bottom, attracting more Cu2+ ions","The suppressor is consumed at the bottom and the leveler plates copper preferentially there","The large, diffusion-limited suppressor (PEG plus Cl-) blocks deposition on the field and trench mouth, while the small accelerator (SPS) adsorbed at the bottom becomes concentrated as the shrinking bottom surface area fills, speeding deposition there","The copper seed is thickest at the via bottom because of PVD line-of-sight deposition"],"answer":2,"explanation":"This is the curvature-enhanced accelerator coverage mechanism: accelerator does not desorb, so as the bottom surface shrinks its coverage rises and deposition accelerates, while the suppressed field stays slow."},{"q":"During copper CMP, what makes the process planarize (remove high points faster than recessed areas) rather than simply thin the copper uniformly?","options":["The pad is harder than copper and only touches the highest features","Slurry oxidizer and complexing agent attack copper everywhere, but a corrosion inhibitor (BTA) passivates the surface; the pad and abrasives strip that passivation only at high points, where removal then proceeds","The eddy-current endpoint sensor stops the polish in recessed areas","The slurry pH is chosen so copper dissolves only under high pressure"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cu CMP is chemically driven and mechanically gated: H2O2 oxidizes, glycine complexes, BTA passivates, and abrasion removes the passivating film only where the pad contacts, so recessed areas are protected."},{"q":"A 30 um long copper line at 1 MA/cm2 does not fail by electromigration in accelerated tests even though longer lines of the same width do. Which effect explains this?","options":["The Blech effect: below a critical J x L product (~2,000 to 4,000 A/cm for Cu), the stress gradient that builds between the compressed anode and tensile cathode ends drives a back-flux that cancels the electron-wind flux","Black's equation predicts lifetime proportional to line length","Short lines have no grain boundaries and therefore no diffusion path","The cobalt cap only works on lines shorter than ~50 um"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At J = 1 MA/cm2 the critical Blech length is ~20 to 40 um; lines shorter than this, bounded by barriers at both ends, are effectively immortal, which design rules exploit."},{"q":"Why is ruthenium a candidate to replace copper at M0/M1 despite having a bulk resistivity of 7.1 uOhm-cm, more than four times copper's 1.68 uOhm-cm?","options":["Ruthenium has a higher melting point, so it can be annealed to larger grains than copper","Ruthenium is cheaper than copper per gram","Ruthenium's short electron mean free path (~6.6 nm) gives it a lower rho x lambda product, and it needs essentially no diffusion barrier, so below roughly 12 to 15 nm line width its total line resistance beats barrier-clad copper; it can also be dry-etched (volatile RuO4), enabling subtractive patterning","Ruthenium has a lower dielectric constant than copper"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The figure of merit at small dimensions is rho x lambda plus the barrier share; Ru wins on both at sub-15 nm widths, and its etchability restores the subtractive metal option with airgaps and no CMP dishing."},{"q":"In the dual damascene flow, why is the plated copper annealed before CMP rather than after it?","options":["The anneal hardens the copper so the pad can abrade it cleanly","As-plated copper is metastable and recrystallizes into larger grains over hours (its resistance drops ~20 percent); a forced anneal at 150 to 400 C completes this first, so the film has a stable removal rate during polishing, grains as large as the trench allows (bamboo structure), and relaxed stress before the cap is deposited","The anneal drives the plating additives out of the copper so they do not contaminate the slurry","CMP must be done on a hot wafer for the BTA inhibitor to work"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Polishing a film that is still recrystallizing gives an unstable removal rate, so the anneal (furnace or hotplate, N2/H2) is done first; it also produces the bamboo grain structure that resists electromigration and relaxes stress before the SiCN cap goes on."},{"q":"Why does moving the power grid to the back of the wafer (backside power delivery) allow the M0 pitch to relax, as Intel reported for PowerVia (30 nm to 36 nm)?","options":["The thinned wafer conducts heat better, so the wires can be spaced further apart","The backside metal carries the signals, so M0 no longer needs to exist","In a conventional stack the power rails occupy M0 tracks alongside every standard-cell row; with power delivered from the back through nano-TSVs those tracks are freed for signals, so fewer tracks are needed per cell and the remaining lines can be spaced wider, which also raised cell utilization by 5 to 10 percent","Backside power removes the need for EUV lithography on M0"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Power rails no longer compete for space on the front side. Intel's PowerVia test vehicle on Intel 4 relaxed M0 from 30 to 36 nm, improved cell utilization by 5 to 10 percent and cut IR drop, while the tight-pitch signal layers are built exactly as before."}]},"13":{"file":"13-metrology-inspection-yield","questions":[{"q":"A spectroscopic ellipsometer reports a 1.85 nm gate oxide with 0.01 nm repeatability. What does the instrument actually measure, and why can its accuracy still be off by several tenths of a nanometer?","options":["It measures the time-of-flight of photons through the film; accuracy is limited by detector timing jitter","It measures the polarization change (Psi and Delta) of obliquely reflected light across many wavelengths and infers thickness by fitting an optical model; if the model (dispersion, interfacial layer, roughness) is wrong, the fitted thickness is wrong regardless of repeatability","It counts atomic layers directly by X-ray diffraction; accuracy is limited by the lattice constant uncertainty","It measures reflectance at a single wavelength; accuracy is limited by lamp intensity drift"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Ellipsometry measures rho = r_p/r_s = tan(Psi) exp(i Delta); thickness comes only from model fitting, which is why recipes are validated against cross-section TEM."},{"q":"In diffraction-based overlay (DBO), two stacked gratings are printed with programmed shifts of +d and -d in two neighboring targets. What is the purpose of the two opposite biases?","options":["To double the target size so the microscope can find it","To allow the proportionality constant between the +1/-1 order intensity asymmetry and the overlay error to be cancelled algebraically, so overlay is obtained without knowing the stack's optical response","To measure overlay in x and y simultaneously with one target","To compensate for resist shrinkage under the electron beam"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The asymmetry between the +1st and -1st diffraction orders is proportional to overlay; with two known biases the unknown sensitivity drops out, giving ~0.1 nm repeatability from a fast, small target."},{"q":"A single-beam e-beam inspector at a 5 nm pixel would need on the order of 2.8 x 10^15 pixels (about 80 days at 400 MHz) to cover a 300 mm wafer. What is the practical consequence, and how do multi-beam tools such as the ASML HMI eScan 1100 address it?","options":["EBI is used only on bare wafers; multi-beam tools add dark-field channels","EBI covers only a small fraction of each wafer on selected care areas; the eScan 1100 splits the source into a 5 x 5 array of 25 beams with separate detectors for up to ~15x single-beam throughput, extending sampling but still not replacing full-wafer optical inspection","EBI is faster than optical inspection at all pixel sizes; multi-beam tools are used for review only","EBI cannot see electrical opens; multi-beam tools add voltage contrast"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Serial pixel scanning limits single-beam EBI to well under 1% of a wafer; 9-beam (eScan 1000) and 25-beam (eScan 1100) arrays multiply throughput but remain sampling tools for critical EUV layers."},{"q":"At D0 = 0.1 defects/cm2, an 800 mm2 GPU die yields about 45% under the Poisson model but about 51% under the negative binomial model with alpha = 2, while a 100 mm2 phone die yields ~90.5% under both. What explains the divergence for the large die?","options":["The negative binomial model assumes defects cluster (gamma-distributed density with clustering parameter alpha), which leaves more large dies defect-free than independent Poisson defects would; the models converge for small dies because A x D0 is small","The Poisson model ignores edge dies, which are more numerous for large dies","The negative binomial model includes systematic yield loss, which is larger for small dies","Large dies have more metal layers, and the Poisson model only counts one layer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Y = (1 + A D0/alpha)^(-alpha) reduces to exp(-A D0) as alpha goes to infinity; real defects cluster, so the Poisson model is pessimistic for large dies, and the choice of alpha matters enormously for GPU economics."},{"q":"The GH100 die (814 mm2) has 144 physical SMs but the H100 SXM product enables only 132. Roughly how does this change the number of sellable dies per 300 mm wafer at D0 = 0.1/cm2, assuming ~70% of the die area is harvestable?","options":["It has no effect on yield; SMs are disabled only for power reasons","It roughly doubles the sellable count, from ~27 to 31 fully defect-free dies (of ~60 to 63 candidates) to ~47 to 49, because only defects in the ~30% non-harvestable area (fabric, memory controllers, NVLink) are fatal","It reduces yield because disabled SMs still consume test time","It raises the count to all ~63 candidates, since any defect can be routed around"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Survival of the ~2.44 cm2 unrepairable region is exp(-0.244) = 78%, and the chance of more than 12 dead SMs in the harvestable region is negligible, so effective yield is ~78% instead of ~45 to 51%."},{"q":"An etch CD has a specification of 18 to 22 nm. The process runs at a mean of 20.2 nm with a standard deviation of 0.5 nm. What is its C_pk, and what is the cheapest way to reach the 1.33 target?","options":["C_pk = 2.0; it already exceeds the target","C_pk = min(22 - 20.2, 20.2 - 18) / (3 x 0.5) = 1.8 / 1.5 = 1.2, below 1.33; centring the mean at 20.0 nm with a recipe offset raises it to 2.0 / 1.5 = 1.33 without reducing the spread","C_pk = 0.6; the only fix is tighter metrology precision","C_pk cannot be computed without the control limits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"C_pk uses the nearer specification limit: (USL - mean)/(3 sigma) = 1.2. Centring costs nothing; the alternative, cutting sigma to about 0.45 nm, is months of process work. C_pk = 1.33 means the nearer limit is 4 sigma from the mean (~60 ppm outside if centred)."},{"q":"Overlaying a metal-1 inspection map on the sort map shows that dies carrying a class-X defect fail 70% of the time, while dies without one fail 30% of the time. What is the kill ratio of class X, and what is it used for?","options":["0.70; it is the fraction of all dies that fail","0.40; it is the difference in failure rates and is used only for reporting","About 0.57, from (0.70 - 0.30)/(1 - 0.30): the extra failures attributable to class X as a fraction of the dies that would otherwise have passed; multiplied by the class-X count per die it gives the yield loss forecast used to disposition a held lot","1.0; any real defect is a killer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The kill ratio removes the baseline failure rate from other causes and normalizes by the dies that would otherwise pass. Kill ratio x defects per die is the yield forecast that decides whether a held lot ships, is reworked, or is scrapped."},{"q":"A WAT via chain of 10^6 vias in series reads open at 5% of the sites on a lot. Assuming independent via failures, what is the per-via open probability, and why does the fab track it?","options":["5 x 10^-2 per via; it is the die yield","About 5 x 10^-8 per via, from p = -ln(0.95)/10^6, because the chain survives with probability exp(-N x p); it is a per-layer input to the defect-density model and a lot-by-lot monitor of which layer's D0 is moving","5 x 10^-6 per via, obtained by dividing 5% by 10^4","It cannot be computed without knowing the via resistance"],"answer":1,"explanation":"One open via anywhere opens the chain, so a 95% chain survival rate over 10^6 vias implies a per-via open rate near 5 x 10^-8. Comb/serpentine structures give the same kind of per-layer defect density, e.g. -ln(0.97)/0.01 cm^2 = 3/cm^2 for 3 failures in 100 sites."}]},"14":{"file":"14-wafer-sort-and-test","questions":[{"q":"Assume a large die has true good-die yield Y = 0.70 in the model and the scan program achieves T = 0.99 fault coverage. Using the Williams-Brown model DL = 1 - Y^(1-T), roughly how many defective parts per million escape, and what happens if coverage is raised to 0.999?","options":["About 300 DPPM; raising coverage to 0.999 has almost no effect because yield dominates","About 3,560 DPPM; raising coverage to 0.999 cuts escapes roughly tenfold to about 360 DPPM","About 10,000 DPPM (1 - T); coverage and yield are independent so raising T to 0.999 gives 1,000 DPPM","About 30 DPPM; raising coverage to 0.999 brings it to zero"],"answer":1,"explanation":"DL = 1 - 0.70^0.01 = 1 - exp(0.01 ln 0.70) = 0.00356, or ~3,560 DPPM; at T = 0.999 it is 1 - 0.70^0.001 = ~357 DPPM. Each additional nine of coverage cuts escapes about tenfold, and lower yield makes escapes worse at the same coverage."},{"q":"Why do probers re-align the wafer to the probe card after heating the chuck to 125 °C, rather than reusing the alignment done at room temperature?","options":["Silicon's ~2.6 ppm/K expansion moves the wafer edge by roughly 78 µm across 300 mm for a 100 K rise, which is larger than a 45 µm pad, and the probe card expands differently","The vacuum clamp weakens at high temperature and the wafer slips on the chuck","The camera optics defocus at high temperature and the original alignment data is lost","The probe tips soften above 100 °C and must be re-seated before each wafer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"300 mm x 2.6e-6/K x 100 K = 78 µm of total expansion, far more than the ±1 µm positioning budget, and the ceramic/PCB probe card expands by a different amount, so a hot alignment after soak is required."},{"q":"Why is simple IDDQ testing (comparing quiescent supply current to a fixed limit) no longer an effective defect screen on advanced-node SoCs, and what replaced it?","options":["Modern testers cannot measure current accurately; it was replaced by scan testing","IDDQ only detects opens, which are rare at advanced nodes; it was replaced by continuity testing","Intrinsic leakage across billions of transistors is amperes and varies across the wafer by more than a defect adds; delta-IDDQ between vector states and statistical outlier screening (PAT/DPAT) replaced fixed limits","IDDQ requires the chip to be powered down, which is impossible on a GPU; it was replaced by burn-in"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A CMOS bridging defect adds a roughly constant current, but at advanced nodes the background leakage swamps it and varies with process corner; delta-IDDQ and comparing each die to its wafer neighbors recover the signal."},{"q":"A GPU sort cell (tester, prober, probe cards, overhead) costs about $0.10 per productive second and tests one die at a time for 240 s across two temperature insertions. A mobile SoC is tested 16 sites in parallel for 25 s on a $0.05 per second cell. What are the approximate per-die sort costs?","options":["GPU ~$2.40, mobile SoC ~$1.25","GPU ~$240, mobile SoC ~$0.80","GPU ~$24, mobile SoC ~$1.25","GPU ~$24, mobile SoC ~$0.08"],"answer":3,"explanation":"GPU: 240 s x $0.10 = ~$24 per die. Mobile SoC: 25 s x $0.05 / 16 sites = ~$0.078 per die. Multi-site parallelism is the main reason small-die test is cheap and single-site GPU test is not."},{"q":"In a package with 8 HBM stacks of 12 DRAM dies each (96 DRAM dies), each DRAM die shipped at 500 DPPM after wafer test, roughly what fraction of packages will contain at least one escaped bad DRAM die, and why does this matter so much for CoWoS?","options":["About 0.05%; it barely matters because HBM stacks can be reworked","About 4.7%; every such package scraps more than $10,000 of good GPU, HBM, interposer, and substrate because bonded stacks cannot be reworked","About 48%; this is why HBM is tested only after stacking","About 0.5%; the interposer's redundancy repairs the bad die"],"answer":1,"explanation":"1 - (1 - 0.0005)^96 = ~4.7%. Thermocompression- and hybrid-bonded stacks have no rework path, so known-good-die quality on every constituent die directly sets the scrap rate on the most expensive assembly in the chain."},{"q":"What is the mechanical purpose of probe overtravel (driving the chuck 50 to 100 µm past first contact), and what does it produce on an aluminum pad?","options":["It compensates for chuck flatness error only; it leaves no mark on the pad","It bends the probes sideways to hit multiple pads per probe, reducing probe count","It compresses each probe spring to generate ~2 to 8 gf of contact force and, on cantilever needles, a 10 to 25 µm scrub that scrapes through the native Al2O3 to reach clean metal","It heats the tip by friction so it can solder itself to the pad for the duration of the test"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Each probe is a spring; overtravel sets its compression and hence its force. The lateral wipe of a deflecting cantilever needle breaks the few-nanometer native oxide, which is why contact resistance drops below ~1 Ω, and the resulting probe mark must stay within the assembly house's pad-damage limits."},{"q":"A chip passes a test with 99% stuck-at fault coverage but later fails during memory training. Which interpretation is best?","options":["The quoted coverage proves the tester was defective","Coverage measures the chosen fault model, while memory training may expose timing, interface or interaction failures outside that model","A 99% coverage test guarantees exactly 1% field failures","Memory interfaces cannot be tested electrically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Fault coverage is coverage of modeled faults. It is not a measured probability of detecting all physical defects or system-level failure modes."},{"q":"A die passes wafer sort. Which new risk justifies electrical test after packaging?","options":["Its logical architecture automatically changes during dicing","Assembly can create opens, shorts or stressed joints and introduces interfaces absent during wafer probing","Wafer sort tests only the package substrate","Passing sort means every final-test failure must be a software bug"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Package assembly adds connections and thermal/mechanical stress. Final test checks the assembled component, while wafer sort protects assembly from already-detectable bad dies."}]},"15":{"file":"15-memory-dram-nand-hbm","questions":[{"q":"A DRAM cell with Cs = 12 fF shares charge with a bit line of Cbl = 36 fF precharged to VDD/2 = 0.55 V. Roughly what signal reaches the sense amplifier, and why can't the capacitor simply shrink with the cell footprint?","options":["~ 550 mV; it can shrink, because the sense amplifier is differential","~ 1.1 V; the word-line boost voltage VPP restores the full signal","~ 12 mV; DRAM works because reads are repeated 64 times per refresh cycle","~ 140 mV; below ~ 60-80 mV the sense amp's offset and bit-line noise cause read errors, so Cs must stay ~ 10-20 fF and the capacitor grows taller instead"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Charge sharing gives ΔV = 0.55 V × 12/(12+36) ≈ 140 mV. Sense amplifiers need a margin above their mismatch offset and coupling noise, so capacitance has been held roughly constant since the mid-2000s while the capacitor's height rose to > 1 µm at aspect ratios above 40."},{"q":"In the 3D NAND gate-replacement flow, when is the tungsten word-line metal actually deposited?","options":["First, as alternating W/oxide layers, before the channel holes are etched","Immediately after the channel-hole etch, before the ONO and polysilicon channel","After the channel, staircase and slit are formed: hot phosphoric acid dissolves the sacrificial silicon nitride layers through the slit, and W (with TiN/Al2O3 liners) is ALD/CVD-filled into the resulting horizontal cavities","During BEOL, together with the copper bit lines"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The stack is deposited as oxide/nitride pairs because nitride can be etched with > 100:1 selectivity in hot H3PO4 and the etch must happen after the channel pillars exist to hold the oxide shelves up; W is then filled from the slit inward and etched back in the slit to separate the layers."},{"q":"Why does HBM4 at its JEDEC base rate deliver ~ 2 TB/s per stack while running each pin at a lower speed (~ 8 Gbps) than HBM3E (~ 9.2 Gbps)?","options":["HBM4 core dies use a faster 1γ DRAM cell with lower latency","HBM4 doubles the interface from 1,024 to 2,048 data bits, so 2,048 × 8 Gbps / 8 ≈ 2.05 TB/s; bandwidth is pins × data rate","HBM4 uses hybrid bonding, which removes the solder joint resistance that limited HBM3E","HBM4 stacks are 16-high, and bandwidth scales with the number of core dies"],"answer":1,"explanation":"HBM is a wide-and-slow interface. HBM3E: 1,024 × 9.2 Gbps ≈ 1.18 TB/s. HBM4: 2,048 × 8 Gbps ≈ 2.05 TB/s. Doubling the width puts the burden on the base die (now made on a foundry logic process) and the interposer routing rather than on per-pin signalling."},{"q":"SK hynix's MR-MUF process won most of NVIDIA's HBM3/HBM3E business over Samsung's TC-NCF. Which statement best describes the mechanism behind that advantage?","options":["MR-MUF forms all solder joints at once in a reflow oven and then injects a filled epoxy molding compound with ~ 2× the thermal conductivity of NCF, giving higher throughput, less force on thin dies, lower warpage and cooler stacks; TC-NCF bonds each die serially under heat and force through a low-conductivity film","MR-MUF uses hybrid Cu-Cu bonding with no bumps, giving a thinner stack","MR-MUF places the base die on a TSMC logic process, which Samsung could not access","MR-MUF thins dies to 30 µm, whereas TC-NCF cannot go below 100 µm"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Thermo-compression bonding dwells each of 12 dies at ~ 250-300 °C under force, with an epoxy film of plastic-like conductivity; mass reflow plus molded underfill parallelizes the joint formation and uses a silica-filled EMC that conducts heat better and holds the stack flatter. Samsung's 12-high HBM3E only passed NVIDIA qualification in September 2025."},{"q":"JEDEC's HBM4 standard sets the package height at 775 µm for both 12-high and 16-high stacks. What did that decision do to the technology roadmap?","options":["It forced immediate adoption of hybrid bonding, because 16 microbumped dies cannot fit","It allowed 16-high stacks to be built with existing microbump TCB/MR-MUF processes by thinning core dies to ~ 30 µm, postponing hybrid bonding to HBM4E/HBM5","It made 16-high stacks impossible until 3D DRAM is available","It had no effect, because HBM height is set by the GPU lid, not by JEDEC"],"answer":1,"explanation":"16 × ~ 30 µm dies + 16 bump gaps of ~ 12 µm + a ~ 50 µm base die + ~ 30 µm of bottom solder is ~ 750 µm, which fits in 775 µm with microbumps with only ~ 25 µm to spare; hybrid bonding would remove ~ 190 µm of gaps but requires sub-nm surface finishes and sub-µm placement that were not production-ready. The relaxed height therefore let vendors ship HBM4 with proven bonding."},{"q":"HBM requires 2.5 times as many wafer starts per shipped bit as conventional DRAM in an illustrative model. If 100,000 existing monthly starts are redirected from DRAM to HBM, what is the correct comparison?","options":["250,000 physical DRAM wafer starts disappear","The redirected wafers produce HBM bits equivalent to about 40,000 conventional-DRAM wafers; total bit output falls by about 60,000 conventional-wafer-equivalents","HBM bit output is 250,000 conventional-wafer-equivalents","No bit output changes because a wafer always holds the same number of useful bits"],"answer":1,"explanation":"100,000 / 2.5 = 40,000. The previous 100,000-wafer conventional output is displaced, so the net total-bit reduction is 60,000 equivalents. The inverse calculation—250,000 starts—applies to producing an HBM bit quantity equal to 100,000 conventional wafers."},{"q":"Why does activating a DRAM row require restoring the stored values as part of sensing?","options":["DRAM reads use ultraviolet light that destroys the oxide","Connecting a cell capacitor to the bit line redistributes its small charge; the sense amplifier resolves and restores the value","The capacitor is deliberately erased to implement wear leveling","Refresh applies only to flash, so no restoration occurs"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Charge sharing produces the small differential voltage used for sensing. The sense amplifier drives the selected cell back toward a clear stored state; the external read does not discard the data."},{"q":"Two 3D NAND products advertise the same layer count. Why might they still have different usable bits per square millimeter?","options":["Only the product name determines density","Channel pitch, active versus support layers, bits per cell, periphery and redundancy can differ","All NAND cell areas are identical by JEDEC rule","Die area depends only on the number of packaging bumps"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Layer count alone omits lateral geometry, state count and overhead. Compare like-for-like active layers and complete die area, then account for usable capacity and yield."}]},"16":{"file":"16-packaging-fundamentals","questions":[{"q":"A 30 mm square flip-chip die on an organic substrate (CTE ~15 ppm/K vs. 2.6 ppm/K for Si) cools from solder solidification at ~217 C to room temperature. Roughly how large is the unconstrained relative displacement at a corner bump, and what package element prevents this from destroying the joint?","options":["~0.5 um; the solder resist absorbs it","~5 um; the copper pillar's ductility absorbs it","~50 um; capillary underfill couples die and substrate so they bend together instead of shearing the bumps","~500 um; the integrated heat spreader clamps the die flat"],"answer":2,"explanation":"DNP of a corner bump is ~21 mm; 21 mm x 12.4 ppm/K x 192 K is ~50 um, a ~70% shear strain across a 70 um bump. Underfill converts that shear into shared warpage, cutting bump strain by more than an order of magnitude."},{"q":"Why do copper pillar bumps replace solder-only C4 bumps as bump pitch shrinks below ~130 um?","options":["Copper is cheaper than tin-silver solder per bump","A solder ball's height scales with its diameter, so fine-pitch solder bumps become too short for underfill and bridge easily, whereas a tall Cu pillar keeps a ~50-70 um stand-off and also resists electromigration far better than Sn","Copper pillars can be wire-bonded while solder bumps cannot","Copper pillars need no under-bump metallurgy"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A plated Cu pillar decouples height from pitch and carries 5-10x higher current density before electromigration; the small SnAg cap still provides the solder joint."},{"q":"In stealth dicing, why is a ~1,064 nm laser used and what actually separates the dies?","options":["Silicon is nearly transparent at 1,064 nm (the photon energy is only just above the band gap), so the beam can be focused inside the wafer to create a plane of internal defects; the dies are then separated by stretching the dicing tape, with zero kerf","1,064 nm is strongly absorbed by silicon, so it ablates a groove from the surface; a blade finishes the cut","1,064 nm heats the dicing tape, which melts and releases the dies","1,064 nm matches the bandgap of the low-k dielectric, vaporizing the scribe street"],"answer":0,"explanation":"At ordinary intensity silicon barely absorbs 1,064 nm light, so the beam reaches the focus inside the wafer; there, nonlinear (multi-photon) absorption creates a modified \"SD layer\" while the surfaces are untouched. Tape expansion of ~10-20% then cleaves along those planes, so there is no kerf, water, or chipping."},{"q":"In the semi-additive process (SAP) for ABF build-up layers, what is the purpose of the permanganate desmear step, and why does ABF contain sub-micron silica filler?","options":["Desmear removes photoresist; the silica lowers the film's cost","Desmear etches the resin skin so the silica particles protrude as a micro-rough surface that electroless copper can adhere to; the filler also sets the film's CTE and stiffness","Desmear polishes the copper flat; the silica makes the film transparent for laser alignment","Desmear activates the palladium catalyst; the silica is a flame retardant"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Copper adhesion to the build-up dielectric depends on the reproducible roughness left after permanganate desmear exposes the filler; this tuned resin-filler system is central to why Ajinomoto's film became the standard."},{"q":"A 10,000-signal die escape needs about 3-4 signal routing layers at 10/10 um line/space on a 150 um bump pitch. Why does moving to 2/2 um line/space reduce the layer count so dramatically?","options":["Finer lines carry more current, so fewer power layers are needed","The number of traces that fit in the ~85 um channel between adjacent via pads rises from ~3 to ~20, so one layer can escape almost all signals, and each removed layer saves a lamination cycle and a few percent of yield","Finer lines allow the bumps to be placed at a coarser pitch","At 2 um the copper can be etched subtractively, which is faster"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Escape routing is limited by traces per channel between via pads: (gap - space)/(line + space). At 10/10 um that is ~3; at 2/2 um it is ~20, so the whole die could escape on one signal layer."},{"q":"An MSL 3 package has exceeded its allowed 168-hour floor life at the specified temperature and humidity and is reflowed without the required moisture handling. What failure risk increases, and why?","options":["Solder balls fail to wet because the ENIG finish oxidizes after 168 hours","Wire bonds fatigue because the mold compound softens above its Tg","Popcorning: absorbed moisture (~0.1-0.3 wt% in the EMC and substrate) vaporizes at an internal interface, and the several-MPa steam pressure delaminates and cracks the package","Tin whiskers grow from the balls because of the extended exposure"],"answer":2,"explanation":"MSL 3 floor life is 168 hours at 30 C/60% RH; beyond that the part must be baked (125 C, 24 h). Water's vapor pressure at 260 C is ~4.7 MPa, enough to delaminate die attach, mold, or underfill interfaces. Exactly one week within the specified conditions is the rated limit, not proof of failure. Baking requirements depend on the package, exposure and applicable handling instructions."},{"q":"A 30 x 27 mm die (8.1 cm^2) dissipates 700 W under a lid. Using R = t/(k*A), which layer of the thermal stack costs the most temperature rise, and roughly how much is recovered by switching from a 100 um polymer TIM (k ~5 W/m*K) to a 100 um indium solder TIM (k ~80 W/m*K)?","options":["The 500 um of silicon (k ~150 W/m*K) dominates at ~17 C; changing the TIM recovers about 1 C","The polymer TIM dominates at ~17 C versus ~3 C for the silicon; indium cuts the TIM to ~1 C, recovering ~15 C","The copper lid dominates because it is thickest; the TIM material makes no difference","All three layers contribute equally, about 5 C each"],"answer":1,"explanation":"R = t/(k*A): silicon 5e-4/(150 x 8.1e-4) = 4.1 mK/W (2.9 C at 700 W); polymer TIM 1e-4/(5 x 8.1e-4) = 25 mK/W (17 C); indium 1e-4/(80 x 8.1e-4) = 1.5 mK/W (1.1 C). The 100 um TIM is the hottest layer in the stack, which is why high-power parts use solder TIM or go lidless with a cold plate on the die."},{"q":"An ABF substrate has an illustrative independent 98% yield for each of 18 build-up stages. Why can eliminating unnecessary stages improve total yield even if the surviving stages do not change?","options":["The stage yields multiply: 0.98^18 is about 70%, while 0.98^12 is about 78%","Yield is always 98% because only the final stage counts","Six fewer stages guarantees a 6% increase in every package lifetime","Removing stages automatically doubles routing density"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Under this simplified independent-stage model each added stage is another opportunity for loss. Real defects may correlate, and removing layers must preserve routing, power and reliability requirements."}]},"17":{"file":"17-advanced-packaging-cowos","questions":[{"q":"NVIDIA moved Blackwell (B200) from CoWoS-S to CoWoS-L. What is the core mechanism by which CoWoS-L lowers cost and stress relative to a full 3.3x-reticle silicon interposer?","options":["It replaces the silicon interposer with a thicker organic substrate whose 8-15 um lines can carry the HBM interface directly","It shrinks each GPU die below the reticle limit so the interposer can be exposed in a single field","It embeds small 65 nm-class LSI silicon bridges only under the die edges that need sub-micron routing, and builds the rest of the interposer as ~2 um organic RDL with copper TIVs for power","It stacks the HBM directly on top of the GPU with hybrid bonding so no interposer wiring is needed"],"answer":2,"explanation":"CoWoS-L keeps fine 0.4 um-class silicon RDL only in the Local Silicon Interconnect bridges where GPU meets HBM or GPU meets GPU; the large-area interposer is molded organic RDL with through-InFO vias. A 3.3x CoWoS-S interposer would have been the most expensive silicon in the package after the GPU dies and worse CTE-matched to the substrate."},{"q":"A Blackwell-class interposer is ~48 x 58 mm with a five-layer RDL, and the scanner field is 26 x 33 mm. How many stitched exposures does one interposer need, and what tolerance does stitching impose?","options":["A 2 x 2 array per layer, so 20 stitched fields per interposer, with field-to-field overlay held well under 100 nm because 0.4 um lines cross the stitch boundaries","A single exposure per layer because interposer RDL is patterned on older 65 nm tools that have larger fields","A 3 x 3 array per layer, so 45 fields, with overlay of a few micrometres, comparable to die-bonder placement accuracy","Two half-field exposures per layer, since High-NA anamorphic optics are used for interposer RDL"],"answer":0,"explanation":"48 x 58 mm exceeds 26 x 33 mm in both directions, so each layer is a 2 x 2 array of overlapping fields; five layers gives twenty stitched fields. Wires drawn across a boundary must overlap so a small overlay error does not open them, which for 0.4 um lines means overlay well under 100 nm. The 3 x 3 array applies to the 5.5x (~60 x 78 mm) generation."},{"q":"In hybrid bonding (SoIC), why does a single 1 um particle at the interface matter so much more than it would in a microbump joint, and what is the consequence for the process?","options":["The particle shorts adjacent copper pads because there is no underfill to insulate them, so a thick dielectric is added","The particle dissolves into the copper during the anneal and forms brittle intermetallics, so the anneal is limited to 150 C","The particle blocks the plasma activation step, so activation is repeated until the surface passes ellipsometry","With no solder to absorb it, the thinned surfaces bend around the particle and leave an unbonded void on the order of a millimetre across, killing every pad inside it; so cleaning, activation, and bonding run in an ISO Class 1 mini-environment with inline void inspection before anneal"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A hybrid bond is a direct oxide-to-oxide and Cu-to-Cu contact with no compliant solder, so a trapped 1 um particle produces a void hundreds to thousands of times its own size. That is why the bond cell is ISO Class 1 and why infrared or acoustic inspection catches voids before the 200-300 C anneal makes them permanent."},{"q":"Compare connection density between a conventional CoWoS microbump array and a 6 um-pitch hybrid bond. Which pair of numbers is correct?","options":["Microbumps at 10-15 um pitch give ~5,000 per mm2; hybrid bonding at 6 um gives ~10,000 per mm2, roughly 2x","Microbumps at 40-55 um pitch (Cu pillar plus SnAg cap) give a few hundred per mm2; hybrid bonding at 6 um gives ~28,000 per mm2, roughly 50-60x a 45 um array","Microbumps at 100-130 um pitch give ~60 per mm2; hybrid bonding at 6 um gives ~1,000 per mm2","Both are around 25 um pitch; hybrid bonding wins only on energy per bit, not density"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Die-to-interposer microbumps sit at 40-55 um pitch (a ~25 um pillar with ~10 um of solder), about 330-500 per mm2 at 45-55 um. Hybrid bonding at 6 um pitch packs ~28,000 connections per mm2 (1/(6 um)^2), roughly 55x the 45 um array and ~470x a 130 um C4 array (~59 per mm2), which is why the stacked-die interface behaves like a wide on-die bus at ~0.1 pJ/bit. 100-130 um is the C4 or UCIe standard-package pitch."},{"q":"Why does the module say known-good die (KGD) and re-testing HBM stacks before placement are economically unavoidable in CoWoS assembly?","options":["Because reflowed microbumps can be reworked only once, so a second failure scraps the module","Because TSMC charges per placement and untested dies raise the tool count required","Because after microbump reflow and underfill there is no rework, so one dead HBM stack or one open joint among ~250,000 microbumps scraps an assembly carrying ~$5,000-7,000 of parts that would have sold for over $30,000","Because HBM stacks are not tested by their makers and CoWoS is the first electrical test they receive"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Once dies are reflowed and underfilled the assembly cannot be taken apart. Two ~$500-600 GPU dies plus eight ~$300-500 HBM3E stacks, interposer and substrate are lost together, so a 95% CoW yield on a 16-per-wafer product loses nearly a whole package per wafer. HBM is shipped as tested known-good stacks and CoWoS re-tests them anyway."},{"q":"A 1,000 W GPU die runs its core logic at ~0.8 V. Why does the module conclude that tens of thousands of its ~100,000 microbumps must be dedicated to power and ground rather than signals?","options":["Because I = P/V gives ~1,250 A, and a 25 um copper pillar can safely carry only ~50-100 mA before electromigration, so supply needs ~12,500-25,000 pillars and the ground return as many again, ~25,000-50,000 bumps before any signal is counted","Because the JEDEC HBM footprint requires one ground bump next to every signal bump on the GPU side as well","Because at 45 um pitch a 25 um pillar is too resistive to carry a 10 Gb/s signal, so most bumps are left unused as spacers","Because the interposer TSVs can only be placed under power pads, so the bump map is set by TSV count"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Current, not I/O count, sets the bump count. 1,000 W / 0.8 V = 1,250 A. A 25 um pillar is limited to tens of milliamps by electromigration (the electron wind that pushes metal atoms downstream and opens a void), so ~12,500-25,000 pillars carry the supply current and the same number return it through ground, ~25,000-50,000 for power alone. The ~13,000 HBM signals and other I/O are a minority of the ~100,000 bumps."},{"q":"A CoWoS-S interposer starts as a 775 um-thick wafer, yet its 10 um-diameter TSVs are etched only ~100 um deep and the wafer is later ground down to ~100 um from the back. Why not etch straight through the full thickness?","options":["Because the Bosch process cannot etch silicon deeper than 100 um regardless of hole width","Because a 10 um hole 775 um deep would have a ~77:1 aspect ratio that cannot be etched straight, lined conformally with oxide, barrier and seed, or plated without voids, whereas a 10:1 hole can; so the holes are etched shallow, wiring is built on top, and the excess silicon is ground away once the molded dies make the wafer rigid","Because the front-side RDL must be built before the TSVs, and the RDL tools cannot handle a wafer with through-holes","Because grinding is cheaper than etching per micrometre, so TSMC etches as little as possible and grinds the rest for cost"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Aspect ratio (depth divided by width) governs everything: a 10:1 hole can be Bosch-etched in ~100-200 cycles, lined with a conformal TEOS oxide, TaN barrier and copper seed, and filled bottom-up in about an hour; a 77:1 hole cannot be lined or filled void-free. The remaining 675 um of silicon exists only for handling strength and is removed in Step 3 after the dies and mold compound have turned the wafer into a rigid tile, which is also why chip-on-wafer precedes the thinning."},{"q":"An organic substrate (CTE ~15 ppm/C) 100 mm on a side carries a silicon interposer (CTE 2.6 ppm/C) through a reflow that swings the temperature by ~220 C. Roughly how much more does the substrate expand than the silicon would, and what is the consequence?","options":["About 2.6 um, small enough that no underfill is needed for CoWoS","About 26 um, which is why microbumps are made ~30 um tall","About 0.26 mm (Δα·ΔT·L = 12 x 10^-6 x 220 x 100 mm), so the bonded stack must bend by hundreds of micrometres, lifting corner C4 joints or squashing central ones unless mold compounds, cores, stiffeners and reflow profiles are engineered against it","About 2.6 mm, which is why the interposer is always smaller than 50 mm"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The differential expansion is the CTE difference (15 - 2.6 ≈ 12 ppm/C) times the temperature swing (220 C) times the length (100 mm): 12 x 10^-6 x 220 x 100 mm ≈ 0.26 mm. The stack is stress-free when the solder freezes and bends as it cools, changing sign at the next reflow, with amplitudes of hundreds of micrometres. Corner joints open if the gap exceeds the bump height when the solder freezes; a domed module bridges its central C4s. Engineers measure it by shadow moire through a reflow profile and qualify with JEDEC temperature cycling."}]},"18":{"file":"18-final-test-burn-in-slt","questions":[{"q":"Using the Arrhenius model with Ea = 0.7 eV, the acceleration factor of a 125 C burn-in relative to 55 C use is about 78. If the use temperature is instead 85 C, roughly what happens to the acceleration factor, and why does this matter for a GPU?","options":["It rises to ~150 because the stress-to-use gap in kelvin is smaller and the exponent scales inversely","It stays ~78 because Arrhenius acceleration depends only on the stress temperature","It falls to ~10, so the same 48 h burn-in covers only ~3 weeks of hot-running field time instead of ~5 months","It falls to ~1 because 85 C is above the activation threshold for defect-driven failure"],"answer":2,"explanation":"AF = exp[(Ea/k)(1/T_use - 1/T_stress)]; raising T_use from 328 K to 358 K shrinks the reciprocal-temperature difference and the exponent, giving AF ~10. Burn-in duration must therefore be set against the customer's real junction temperature."},{"q":"Which of the following can be tested for the first time at package-level final test rather than at wafer sort, and for what physical reason?","options":["Scan-chain stuck-at faults, because scan requires the package's power planes","The GPU-to-HBM interface and NVLink SerDes at 200 Gb/s, because the HBM stacks did not exist at sort and a probe card cannot pass those data rates or full current","SRAM repair fuses, because fuses cannot be programmed until the package lid is attached","Transistor threshold voltage, because it changes during reflow"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 1,024-bit-per-stack HBM bus, the die-to-die NV-HBI link and at-speed PAM4 SerDes require the assembled package and a controlled-impedance socket; probe cards are limited in current per needle and are impedance discontinuities at those rates."},{"q":"A back-end flow has assembly yield 98%, FT1 96%, burn-in plus FT2 99.2%, SLT 98.5% and module test 99%. Starting from 1,000 sorted die, approximately how many modules ship, and which single stage costs the most in dollars?","options":["~910 modules; FT1, because it has the largest fallout on a package already worth ~$3,000","~960 modules; assembly, because it has the lowest yield of the chain","~910 modules; SLT, because system-level test slots are the most expensive equipment","~850 modules; burn-in, because the oven damages good parts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"0.98 x 0.96 x 0.992 x 0.985 x 0.99 = 0.910. FT1 removes ~39 units at ~$3,000 each (~$117k), more than any other stage, because the fallout is highest and the full package value including HBM is already at risk. The yields are the module's illustrative industry ranges, not published NVIDIA data; the compounding arithmetic is what carries over."},{"q":"Why does a system-level test (SLT) insertion catch failures that a scan program with 99% stuck-at coverage misses?","options":["SLT runs at a higher temperature than the ATE can reach","Scan coverage is measured against a fault model, while SLT boots real firmware and exercises clock-domain crossings, power-management state transitions and analog-firmware interactions that no fault model represents","SLT applies a higher voltage stress than burn-in","SLT tests the HBM stacks, which scan cannot access"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Structural test targets modelled faults on synchronous logic; SLT is the only screen that runs the product as a system, which is why its fallout on GPUs (~0.5-2%) consists almost entirely of parts that passed every ATE block."},{"q":"In an HBM stack, how is each DRAM die tested after stacking, when a probe can no longer reach it?","options":["Each die is probed through dedicated test TSVs that run to the top of the stack","The stack is X-rayed and any die with visible defects is remapped","The base die provides an IEEE 1500 wrapper for serial access to every die's test registers and a direct-access port that exposes a reduced-width interface for array testing, plus MBIST and TSV/lane repair logic","It is not tested until the stack is on the CoWoS interposer, because the 1,024-bit interface is required"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The JEDEC HBM standard places test access in the base die: IEEE 1500 for per-die control and repair programming, and a DA port so the full array can be tested through a few hundred pads before the wide interface exists."},{"q":"A clean HTOL result (3 lots x 77 units, 1,000 h at 125 C, zero failures) with an acceleration factor of ~78 to use temperature gives roughly what upper-bound failure rate at 60% confidence?","options":["~5 FIT","~51 FIT","~500 FIT","~5,000 FIT"],"answer":1,"explanation":"231 x 1,000 x 78 = 18.0 million equivalent device-hours; with zero fails, chi-squared(0.60, 2 dof)/2 = 0.916, so lambda <= 0.916 / 18.0e6 per hour = 5.1e-8 per hour = ~51 failures per 1e9 device-hours."},{"q":"A GPU read IDDQ = 22.0 A at FT1 and 28.6 A at FT2 after burn-in. The absolute IDDQ limit is 35 A and the FT2 delta limit is +20%. What is the verdict, and why is a per-unit delta a better screen than an absolute limit here?","options":["Pass: 28.6 A is inside the 35 A absolute limit, and delta limits are only advisory","Fail: the +30% rise exceeds the delta limit; a per-unit comparison is needed because the healthy population's IDDQ spread across process corners (roughly +/-40%) is wider than the 20-30% drift of a degrading part, so no absolute limit can catch the drift without also failing healthy high-leakage units","Fail: any IDDQ rise after burn-in proves a defect, so the delta limit should be 0%","Pass: burn-in permanently raises leakage in every part by about 30%, so the rise is expected"],"answer":1,"explanation":"28.6 / 22.0 = 1.30, a 30% rise, above the +20% delta limit though inside the 35 A absolute limit. FT2 looks up the same unit's FT1 record by ECID in the STDF database. If every unit in the lot had risen by ~25%, the diagnosis would be an over-stressed burn-in recipe or a mis-set FT2 thermal head (checked with golden units), not a lot of degrading parts."},{"q":"A qualification sample completes accelerated life testing with zero failures. Which conclusion is justified?","options":["The product has zero field failure rate","Under the assumed stress model and sampling assumptions, the result supports a statistical upper bound on failure rate","The activation energy of every failure mechanism must be 0.7 eV","Burn-in can be removed from production without further evidence"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A finite zero-failure sample does not prove zero risk. Confidence bounds depend on exposure, sample size and model validity; different mechanisms may accelerate differently."}]},"19":{"file":"19-building-an-nvidia-gpu","questions":[{"q":"For an 814 mm2 GH100 die, an illustrative D0 = 0.1/cm2 model gives about 45% perfect-die yield. How can a model of sellable yield be higher when the product enables fewer SMs than physically exist?","options":["TSMC's 4N process has a defect density an order of magnitude below the industry norm","The die is built with 144 SMs and 6 HBM controllers but ships with 132 SMs and 5 HBM stacks enabled, so dies with defects in the spare SMs or one memory partition are still sellable","NVIDIA uses a two-die design so that only one of the pair must be defect-free","Wafer sort rejects only dies with defects in the L2 cache, and all other defects are corrected in firmware"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Designed-in redundancy can allow suitable dies with localized defects to be sold with resources disabled. The 65–80% and 40–50 dies-per-wafer figures in the module are illustrative modeling results, not disclosed NVIDIA production yield. Defect location, clustering and non-redundant regions still matter."},{"q":"In the physical design flow, what does DRC/LVS signoff (typically in Siemens Calibre) verify that synthesis, place-and-route, and static timing analysis do not?","options":["That every polygon obeys the foundry's geometric rules (widths, spacings, densities, multi-patterning constraints) and that the transistors and wires extracted from the layout match the gate-level netlist","That all setup and hold timing paths meet the clock period across process corners","That IR drop from the C4 bumps to the transistors stays within ~5-10% of Vdd","That the RTL is functionally equivalent to the C++ performance model"],"answer":0,"explanation":"DRC checks the geometry against the PDK rule deck; LVS proves the layout implements the netlist. Timing is PrimeTime/Tempus, IR drop is RedHawk/Voltus, and functional equivalence is checked by simulation, emulation, and formal tools."},{"q":"Why does Blackwell B200 require CoWoS-L rather than the CoWoS-S used for H100?","options":["CoWoS-L supports HBM3E while CoWoS-S only supports HBM3","The two reticle-limited dies plus eight HBM stacks need ~3.3 reticles of interposer, at which a whole-silicon reticle-stitched interposer becomes too costly and low-yielding, so TSMC puts silicon only where fine wiring is needed: small silicon bridges (LSI) embedded in an organic RDL interposer under the die-to-die and HBM edges, carrying the 10 TB/s NV-HBI link","CoWoS-L is cheaper per package, which offsets the higher HBM cost","CoWoS-S cannot dissipate more than 700 W"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CoWoS-S interposers are already reticle-stitched (H100's is ~2.5 reticles of silicon); at ~3.3 reticles a whole-silicon slab's cost and defect-driven yield loss become prohibitive, and only the strips under the die-to-die and die-to-HBM edges need silicon-fine wiring. CoWoS-L therefore uses an organic RDL interposer with local silicon bridges only at those edges. It is more expensive per package than CoWoS-S, but it is the practical way to build a ~3.3-reticle package."},{"q":"The GB200 NVL72 uses roughly 5,000 passive copper cables (about 2 miles) for its NVLink spine instead of optical links. What is the primary engineering consequence of that choice?","options":["The rack can be air-cooled because copper cables generate no heat","The NVLink domain must extend across many racks because copper has longer reach than fiber","Passive copper at 200 Gb/s per lane reaches only ~1-1.5 m, which confines the 72-GPU NVLink domain to one rack and drives the ~120-140 kW power density that forces direct liquid cooling, in exchange for saving ~20 kW of transceiver power and a large fraction of the cost","Copper limits NVLink bandwidth to 900 GB/s per GPU"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The reach limit of copper is the reason NVL72 is a single very dense rack. Avoiding thousands of ~15-20 W optical transceivers saves power and money, but concentrating 72 GPUs, 36 CPUs, and 18 switch chips in one enclosure makes cold plates and CDUs mandatory."},{"q":"Public BOM estimates put an H100 SXM at ~$3,000-3,500 to manufacture against a ~$25-30k price. Which statement about the cost structure is correct?","options":["The GPU die is the largest cost because a 4N wafer costs ~$16-17k","The five HBM3 stacks (~$1,300-2,000) are the largest item, CoWoS plus substrate is ~$500-1,000, and the GPU die is only ~$300-400, about 10% of the total","Test and burn-in exceed the cost of the die and memory combined","The SXM board and VRM are roughly half of the total"],"answer":1,"explanation":"In this illustrative public cost breakdown, memory and packaging exceed the compute die cost. These estimates do not establish NVIDIA product-level COGS or gross margin, and a cost share does not by itself establish which factory resource limits output."},{"q":"An H100 SM runs at ~1.8 GHz, so the clock period is ~555 ps. Why do designers have only ~465 ps of that for logic on each path, and what is the escalating sequence of fixes when a path does not fit?","options":["The clock is only 84% duty cycle; failing paths are fixed by raising the supply voltage in the VRM firmware","The flip-flop's clock-to-output delay (~40 ps), the next flip-flop's setup margin (~20 ps), and clock skew plus jitter (~30 ps) are subtracted first; a failing path is fixed by upsizing cells, inserting repeaters, re-placing cells closer together, and, as a last resort, changing the RTL, which restarts the emulation compile","Parasitic extraction adds a fixed 90 ps to every path; failing paths are fixed by adding another metal layer to the PDK","The emulator can only run at 465 ps per cycle; failing paths are fixed by moving them to a slower clock domain"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Only the time between one flip-flop's output becoming valid and the next flip-flop's setup deadline is available for gates and wires, and skew and jitter eat into it. At ~15-25 ps per 4N gate that is ~20-30 levels of logic per cycle, and a wire across a 1-2 mm SM can cost ~100 ps on its own, which is why blocks are kept small and why timing closure is the largest schedule risk in physical design."},{"q":"In 2023 the quoted lead time for an H100 reached ~52 weeks. What was that 52 weeks actually made of?","options":["52 weeks of wafer processing, because an 80-mask-layer N4 flow takes about a year","About 6 months of physical cycle time from wafer start to a GPU on a board, with the remainder being queueing for CoWoS packaging and HBM allocation and the ~12-month capacity commitments the chain requires","26 weeks in the fab and 26 weeks of burn-in and system-level test","Mostly the ~3-month tape-out-to-first-silicon delay repeated for each stepping"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Cycle time (how long a unit of material spends moving through the chain) is ~6 months to a GPU on a board and ~7+ to a burned-in rack. Lead time was longer because HBM wafers, substrates and CoWoS slots must be primed months ahead and NVIDIA commits to TSMC wafer and CoWoS capacity a year or more out; the queue was for allocation, not processing."},{"q":"A GB200 NVL72 rack draws ~130 kW and ~85% of that heat leaves through the liquid loop. Water carries 4.18 kJ per kg per degree C. If the coolant is allowed to warm by 10 degrees C between inlet and outlet, roughly what flow does one rack need?","options":["~16 L/min, about a kitchen tap","~160 L/min (about 2.6 kg/s), roughly a bathtub of water per minute","~1,600 L/min, which is why NVL72 needs a dedicated facility water main per rack","~2 L/min, since most of the heat is removed by the CDU's refrigeration cycle"],"answer":1,"explanation":"~110 kW into the water divided by (4.18 kJ/kg-K x 10 K) gives ~2.6 kg/s, about 160 L/min per rack; 770 racks in a 100 MW site move ~2 m3/s. The CDU is a heat exchanger and pump set, not a refrigerator, and the flow arithmetic is what sizes the manifolds, pumps and pipes of a liquid-cooled data center."}]},"20":{"file":"20-economics-and-geopolitics","questions":[{"q":"A 40,000 wspm leading-edge fab cost $20 billion and depreciates its tools over 5 years. If utilization falls from 90% to 50%, what happens to the depreciation cost per wafer, and why does this matter for foundry behavior?","options":["It roughly doubles (from ~$8k to ~$14k per wafer), which is why foundries discount older nodes to keep fabs loaded rather than idle them","It stays the same because depreciation is a fixed cost that does not depend on output","It falls, because fewer wafers mean less tool wear and lower maintenance","It rises by about 10%, a minor effect compared with materials costs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Annual depreciation (~$3.4B) is fixed, so spreading it over 240,000 instead of 432,000 wafers raises the per-wafer charge from ~$7,900 to ~$14,200. This operating leverage is why utilization, not price, drives foundry margins through the cycle."},{"q":"Why has cost per transistor stopped falling meaningfully since roughly the 28 nm node, even though logic density still improves ~1.5–1.7x per node?","options":["Because foundries now use only EUV, which makes every layer more expensive than DUV","Because wafer price rises ~1.5–1.8x per node, SRAM bit cells stopped shrinking (N3E ~0.021 um^2, same as N5), and analog/I/O do not scale, so most of the die does not get cheaper","Because transistor counts have hit the reticle limit and can no longer grow","Because design costs are now included in wafer prices by the foundries"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Wafer price growth now cancels the density gain, and roughly half the area of a modern CPU or GPU (SRAM, analog, I/O) barely scales at all, which is why chiplets moved those blocks to older nodes."},{"q":"The October 2023 update to the US export controls replaced the interconnect-bandwidth test of the October 2022 rule with a performance-density metric. What loophole did that close?","options":["Chinese firms had been buying EUV scanners through Singapore intermediaries","NVIDIA had built the A800 and H800 by capping interconnect bandwidth at 400 GB/s on otherwise identical dies, keeping them under the 600 GB/s threshold while retaining full compute","Hyperscalers had been renting H100 capacity to Chinese customers from data centers in Malaysia","SMIC had been importing immersion DUV tools classified as 28 nm equipment"],"answer":1,"explanation":"The 2022 rule caught chips with TPP >= 4800 and interconnect >= 600 GB/s; A800/H800 ducked the second test. The 2023 rule keys on TPP and TPP per mm^2 alone, which is what forced the far weaker H20 (TPP ~2400)."},{"q":"What manufacturing tradeoff follows when a foundry uses DUV multi-patterning to make finer logic features, and what can a chip teardown establish?","options":["The foundry cannot obtain silicon wafers pure enough for advanced logic","Every transistor must use a different EDA tool because the exposure wavelength changed","Additional patterning and alignment steps can increase cost, cycle time and defect exposure; a teardown can establish dimensions and structures, but does not establish factory yield or cost per good die","The foundry must switch from 300 mm to 200 mm wafers to use additional exposures"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Splitting a fine pattern across several DUV patterning steps creates extra processing and alignment work. TechInsights identified SMIC N+2 and a further-scaled N+3 from physical chips; its N+3 comparison did not establish equivalence to TSMC or Samsung 5 nm. Production yield, utilization and cost require separate evidence, so a teardown alone cannot prove that a process is uneconomic."},{"q":"Why does geographic concentration in advanced logic and TSMC's CoWoS production create a supply risk even when no fab equipment is physically damaged?","options":["Factories can use any locally available chemicals without qualification, so only damaged equipment stops production","An interruption to qualified chemicals, gases, spare parts or transport can stop output; alternative foundries and packaging technologies require product-specific redesign and qualification","A foundry's headquarters determines where every wafer and package it sells is physically manufactured","All advanced packaging is the same as CoWoS, so another OSAT can take over the process immediately"],"answer":1,"explanation":"CoWoS is TSMC's process family, while other suppliers offer different advanced-packaging platforms. A supply interruption can idle intact production lines. The operational risk depends on actual fab and packaging locations, inventory, logistics and qualified alternatives, not a single market-share percentage or the corporate headquarters."},{"q":"The CHIPS Act's Section 48D investment tax credit was raised from 25% to 35% in the July 2025 tax law. In the worked example of moving ~12,500 wspm of N3 capacity to Arizona, what is the net effect of US incentives on capex versus Taiwan, and where does the persistent US cost penalty actually sit?","options":["Net US capex is still ~50% above Taiwan because tools cost more in the US; the penalty is tool pricing","Net US capex ends up below Taiwan's unsubsidized figure because tools (~80% of capex) cost the same everywhere and the credit plus grants offset the ~1.5x construction premium; the persistent penalty is ~5–20% higher operating cost per wafer","Incentives exactly cancel the construction premium, leaving costs identical; the penalty is entirely in mask-set costs","Incentives do not apply to foreign-headquartered firms like TSMC, so the full 1.5x premium remains"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Only the ~20% building share carries the construction premium; the 35% credit on qualifying property and pro-rata grants more than offset it. The lasting gap is per-wafer operating cost (labor, imported chemicals, scale), which TSMC recovers by pricing US-origin wafers higher."},{"q":"Under the October 2023 US export rule, total processing performance (TPP) is peak operations per second in trillions multiplied by the bit-width of those operations, and performance density is TPP divided by die area. An H100 delivers ~989 TFLOPS of dense 16-bit math on an 814 mm^2 die; the H20 uses the same die at ~148 TFLOPS. Which statement is correct?","options":["H100: TPP ~15,800 and density ~19 TPP/mm^2, over both the 4,800 and 5.92 lines; H20: TPP ~2,370 and density ~2.9, under the control lines and just under the 2,400 notification band, which is why it was exportable","Both chips have the same TPP because they share a die; only interconnect bandwidth differs","H20 fails the density test because it uses the full 814 mm^2 die","TPP is measured in memory bandwidth, so the H20 with full HBM3 is controlled and the H100 is not"],"answer":0,"explanation":"TPP = ~989 x 16 = ~15,800 for H100 (density ~15,800 / 814 = ~19 TPP/mm^2); TPP = ~148 x 16 = ~2,370 for H20 (density ~2.9). The H20 keeps H100 memory but ~15% of its compute, which is why it was designed to sit just under the 2,400 band."},{"q":"An illustrative Blackwell production plan requires about 200,000 logic wafers and 350,000 packaging-wafer equivalents per year. What can comparison with a foundry's total wafer output establish?","options":["A small percentage of total output proves the required advanced node has spare capacity","The physical requirements can be estimated, but bottlenecks also depend on qualified node capacity, allocation, HBM, package mix, yield and test","Packaging wafers are interchangeable with logic wafers","The highest-cost component must always be the bottleneck"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Company-wide wafer totals mix nodes and customers. They do not show available capacity for this product. Compare each requirement with its specific qualified, allocated resource before drawing a bottleneck conclusion."}]},"21":{"file":"21-glossary-and-reference","questions":[{"q":"In the glossary, what does MOL (Middle of Line) refer to?","options":["The 15-18 copper/low-k interconnect levels built after the first contact level","The contact levels between the transistors and M1: trench silicide, source/drain contacts (W, Co, or Ru), gate contacts, and M0","The mid-wafer inspection point between FEOL and BEOL where WAT parametric tests are run","The middle deck of a string-stacked 3D NAND array"],"answer":1,"explanation":"MOL is the set of contact levels that connect the FEOL transistors to the first true BEOL wiring level. The copper/low-k stack above it is the BEOL; WAT is an end-of-line parametric test, not a process stage."},{"q":"Using the sense-of-scale table, order these from largest to smallest: the tightest N3/N2 metal pitch, the contacted poly pitch, the EUV wavelength, and a killer particle.","options":["EUV wavelength > metal pitch > killer particle > contacted poly pitch","Metal pitch > EUV wavelength > contacted poly pitch > killer particle","Killer particle > contacted poly pitch > metal pitch > EUV wavelength","Contacted poly pitch (~45-48 nm) > killer particle (~20-30 nm) roughly equal to metal pitch (~23-25 nm) > EUV wavelength (13.5 nm)"],"answer":3,"explanation":"CPP is ~45-48 nm, the tightest metal pitch is ~23-25 nm, a killer particle is anything larger than about half the metal pitch (~20-30 nm), and the EUV wavelength is 13.5 nm. The scanner prints features larger than its wavelength only by virtue of k1 near 0.3 and NA 0.33-0.55."},{"q":"The node table shows contacted poly pitch (CPP) essentially flat at ~45-50 nm from TSMC N5 through N2 and A16. Why, and where does remaining density gain come from?","options":["CPP must fit a gate, two spacers, and a contact, so it has hit a physical floor; density now comes from metal-pitch scaling, cell-height reduction (fewer tracks, backside power), and DTCO","CPP is limited by the 0.33 NA EUV resolution of ~13.5 nm, so only High-NA tools can shrink it; density gains come from FinFET fin count","Foundries deliberately hold CPP constant so PDKs remain compatible across nodes; density comes from larger reticles","CPP stopped scaling because copper resistivity rises sharply below 50 nm; density now comes from stacking two logic dies with SoIC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A gate pitch has to contain the gate length, two dielectric spacers, and a source/drain contact, and each of those has stopped shrinking. Since N5 the density gain per node (~1.15-1.3x) has come from metal pitch, track reduction, backside power delivery, and design-technology co-optimization such as SDB and COAG."},{"q":"According to the node table and its commentary, what happened to wafer price and lithography's share of cost between TSMC N5 and N2?","options":["Wafer price fell from ~$30k to ~$16-17k as EUV matured, and litho's share dropped to ~25%","Wafer price stayed at ~$10k while EUV layer count fell from ~25 to ~14","Wafer price rose from ~$16-17k (N5) to ~$30k (N2) as EUV layers went from ~14 to ~25+, and litho's share of wafer cost grew from ~25% at N16 to ~35-40% at N2","Wafer price rose to ~$30k solely because N2 uses High-NA EUV on every critical layer"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The table lists N5 at ~$16-17k with ~14 EUV layers and N2 at ~$30k with ~25+ EUV layers including EUV LELE on M0 and vias. Each EUV layer costs 2-3x a DUV layer in tool time, so litho's share climbed from ~25% at N16 to ~35-40% at N2, and cost per transistor stopped falling."},{"q":"The glossary defines Little's law as WIP = throughput x cycle time. A fab running 100,000 wafer starts per month with a 90-day cycle time carries roughly how many wafers in process?","options":["~100,000 wafers, one month's worth","~300,000 wafers, three months' worth","~900,000 wafers, since each wafer passes through ~9 re-entrant loops","~30,000 wafers, since lots of 25 are counted as single units"],"answer":1,"explanation":"100,000 wafers per month times a 3-month (90-day) cycle time gives ~300,000 wafers in process at any moment. Re-entrant flow explains why the cycle time is 90 days for 1,000-1,500 steps but does not multiply the WIP count."},{"q":"The glossary lists three die-yield models. Which statement correctly matches them?","options":["Poisson is the industry default; Murphy is the most pessimistic; negative binomial ignores defect clustering","Negative binomial is the most pessimistic; Poisson includes a clustering parameter alpha; Murphy is used only for memory","Murphy Y = e^(-A*D0) is the simplest; Poisson uses the squared bracket form; both assume clustering","Poisson Y = e^(-A*D0) is the simplest and most pessimistic; Murphy Y = [(1 - e^(-A*D0))/(A*D0)]^2 is less pessimistic for large dies; negative binomial Y = (1 + A*D0/alpha)^(-alpha) with alpha ~2-5 accounts for clustering and is the industry default"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Poisson assumes random, unclustered defects and gives the lowest yield; Murphy softens it for large dies; the negative binomial adds a clustering parameter alpha (typically 2-5) and is what foundries actually use. All three take die area A and killer defect density D0 (~0.05-0.1 /cm2 at a mature leading node) as inputs."},{"q":"The module quotes four numbers: the ~45-48 nm contacted poly pitch, the ~23-25 nm minimum metal pitch, the ~10 nm Rayleigh resolution of 0.33 NA EUV (0.25 x 13.5 nm / 0.33), and the ~26-30 nm fin pitch. Which one is a half-pitch rather than a pitch?","options":["The ~45-48 nm contacted poly pitch, because it is measured from gate center to gate center","The ~23-25 nm minimum metal pitch, because M0 lines are printed by EUV double patterning","The ~10 nm Rayleigh figure, because k1 x lambda / NA gives the minimum half-pitch (one line width when lines and spaces are equal); the other three are center-to-center pitches","The ~26-30 nm fin pitch, because fins are narrower than the spaces between them"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The Rayleigh criterion, resolution = k1 x lambda / NA, gives a half-pitch: 0.25 x 13.5 nm / 0.33 is about 10.2 nm, and 0.33 NA EUV resolves ~13 nm half-pitch in production, which is the same statement as ~26 nm pitch. CPP, metal pitch and fin pitch are all center-to-center repeat distances, i.e. full pitches. Check which is meant before comparing numbers."},{"q":"Why does EUV lithography suffer more stochastic (random) pattern failures than ArF lithography at similar doses?","options":["Each EUV mirror reflects only ~70% of the light, so the aerial image reaching the wafer is blurrier than an ArF lens image","An EUV photon carries ~14x the energy of an ArF photon, so a 20 mJ/cm2 EUV dose is only ~14 photons per nm2 versus ~290 per nm2 for ArF at 30 mJ/cm2; a 10 x 10 nm contact receives only ~1,400 photons, whose square-root shot noise is ~3%, and the rare hole that receives far fewer than average does not open","EUV resists are not chemically amplified, so every photon must clear a resist site on its own","The 13.5 nm wavelength is smaller than the resist polymer molecules, so the image cannot be resolved chemically"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A dose is energy per area, and photon energy scales as 1/wavelength: a 13.5 nm photon is 92 eV and a 193 nm photon 6.4 eV. At production doses EUV therefore puts down roughly 20x fewer photons per nm2 than ArF, and the random arrival of photons (shot noise, proportional to the square root of the count) becomes a visible fraction of the exposure in a 10 x 10 nm feature. Mirror losses affect throughput, not shot noise per unit dose, and most EUV resists are chemically amplified."}]}};
